pub(crate) static PAGES: &[(&str, &str)] = &[
    ("changelog", "# Changes by version\n\n## Unreleased\n\n- Fix remote API-key sign-in outcome reporting. See [remote sign-in](commands.md#remote-sign-in).\n- Persist turn identities before acknowledging queued work. Cancelling a queued turn no longer invalidates later journal entries, and restarting a session cannot reuse its reserved identity. Queued text is not restored.\n- New turn-ID reservations use journal format 2; other records retain format 1. Existing journals need no conversion. A reader must support format 2 after a reservation is written. See [durable turn identities](sessions.md#durable-turn-identities).\n- Restore the public `patch::styles::tier` function and its constant-evaluation support.\n- Reject unsupported Unicode duration units with a configuration error instead of panicking.\n- Report incomplete patch rollback with the affected paths and retained backup locations. Continue restoring other files when one restoration fails.\n\n## 0.1.0 (2026-09-26)\n\n- First v0 release: the headless loop, the terminal, Starlark plugins, the wire surfaces, and this manual.\n"),
    ("cli", "# The command line\n\nRun `dalgon --help` for the full flag list. Print mode runs one turn, then waits up to 3 seconds for its extension status kinds to go quiet. JSON mode (`--json`) writes a result as one JSON line only after the turn ends and every extension status kind is quiet, so a run that leaves an extension busy holds the result line back.\n\nPrint mode reports `dalgon: extension status is not quiet: <extensions> still busy after 3 seconds` on stderr and exits 1 if a kind remains busy after the quiet wait. JSON mode has no time limit: it waits for quiet or a stop signal; stopping ends the run without writing a JSON result line.\n\n## dal docs\n\nRead the embedded manual. Usage: `dalgon docs [URI]`. With no URI, prints the index. With a page URI, prints the page. Unknown URIs fail with a miss message on stderr and nothing on stdout.\n\n## dal login\n\nSign in to a model provider, then pick a model.\n\nRun `dalgon login status` to inspect stored credentials and configured API keys.\nThe command exits 0 when at least one provider is ready. If all credentials are\nmissing or expired, it exits 1 and prints a sign-in hint. OAuth rows show their\nexpiry date. The output does not include tokens or API keys.\n\n## dal rpc\n\nServe the JSON-RPC protocol over stdio or a local socket.\n\n## dal serve\n\nStart the model router and agent-to-agent endpoint. Listens on loopback only; `--public` requires a token for remote clients arriving through a trusted HTTPS/WSS reverse proxy on the same host.\n\n## dal plugin\n\nManage plugins: list, grant, revoke.\n\n## dal completion\n\nPrint shell completions for the named shell.\n\nFILES: config lives under the per-OS config root; data lives under the per-OS data root.\n\nENVIRONMENT: `DAL_LOG` selects the log level. `DAL_NO_MOTION` disables terminal motion, and `DAL_DEBUG` enables terminal diagnostics. Other `DAL_` names do not override config keys. Use `dal.toml` or command-line flags to change settings.\n\nExit codes: 0 success, 1 requested failure, 124 usage, 125 internal error; interrupted runs exit 130; broken pipes exit 141. Every error names what happened and the fix.\n"),
    ("commands", "# Slash commands\n\nBuilt-in commands, in registry order. Plugin commands appear after these with their own names.\n\n| command | hint | summary |\n|---|---|---|\n| /settings |  | view and change settings |\n| /model | [provider/model] | pick the model |\n| /tree |  | navigate the session tree |\n| /thinking | [level] | set the thinking level |\n| /scoped-models |  | choose the models that /model lists |\n| /export | [path] | write the session to a .md or .jsonl file |\n| /import | <path> | open a session from a .jsonl file |\n| /share |  | not in dalgon: use /export |\n| /bug |  | not in dalgon: use /export and /session |\n| /copy |  | copy the last reply to the clipboard |\n| /name | [name] | set or show the session name |\n| /session |  | show session details and usage |\n| /changelog |  | show what changed in each version |\n| /hotkeys |  | show keyboard shortcuts |\n| /fork |  | new branch from a previous message |\n| /clone |  | copy this session into a new one |\n| /trust |  | not in dalgon: plugins load only from the data directory |\n| /login | [provider] | sign in to a provider |\n| /logout | [provider] | remove stored credentials |\n| /new |  | start a new session |\n| /compact | [instructions] | summarize older context now |\n| /resume | [id or name] | open another session |\n| /reload |  | reload plugins |\n| /quit |  | quit dalgon |\n| /mode | <normal\\|eval-first\\|eval-only> | set the harness mode |\n\n`/share`, `/bug`, and `/trust` never upload. `/share` directs you to `/export`, which writes a file you can send yourself. A conflicting command name is an error `command \"<name>\" is already registered by plugin \"<first>\"; plugin \"<second>\" cannot register it`; the numeric-suffix form `/review:1` is rejected. Completion matches name prefixes and orders them by name.\n\n## Remote sign-in\n\nEsc cancels a remote API-key sign-in only before you submit the key. After submission, wait for the server’s result. If it succeeds, use `/logout` to remove the stored credential.\n\n## Skills\n\nA skill is not a slash command, and `/` completion never lists `skill:` entries. Run one with `/skill:<name> [text]`, which sends `text` with it, or with a `$` token in any prompt.\n\n- `/skill <name>` runs the skill when the name is exact. Any other text lists the skills whose name contains it and runs nothing: `/skill find-` prints every candidate and starts no turn.\n- In the composer, a token that starts with `$` opens a skills-only completion list with the same match and order as `/`. Tab puts the first match in place of that token.\n- A prompt that holds the whole token `$<name>`, `$<plugin>:<name>`, or `/skill:<name>` runs that skill, and a prompt may name several. The body of each named skill joins the turn once, after your message, in order of first mention. Any other token stays text.\n"),
    ("config", "# Settings in dal.toml\n\nThe file is `dal.toml` under `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dal/` (default `~/.config/dal/dal.toml`); data lives under `$XDG_DATA_HOME/dal/`. Layering: code defaults, then product defaults, then user `dal.toml`, then CLI flags. A later layer replaces an earlier value by key path. `dalgona` reads `dal.toml` under `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/dalgona/` with data under `$XDG_DATA_HOME/dalgona/`.\n\nThe table shows dal's built-in defaults before product TOML, user settings, and CLI overrides. The meaning column identifies dalgona's different defaults. `none` means omit the key, not the TOML string `\"none\"`; an omitted table still uses the field defaults listed below. Replace `<data-root>` with your product's data directory.\n\n| key | type | default | meaning |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `mode` | string | `normal` | the `mode` setting |\n| `model` | string | `none` | the `model` setting |\n| `thinking` | string | `medium` | the `thinking` setting |\n| `approval` | string | `ask` | the `approval` setting |\n| `screen` | string | `inline` | the `screen` setting |\n| `theme` | string | `auto` | the `theme` setting |\n| `sandbox` | bool | `false` | the `sandbox` setting |\n| `images` | bool | `false` | the `images` setting |\n| `motion` | bool | `true` | the `motion` setting |\n| `compact_ratio` | number | `0.85` | the `compact_ratio` setting |\n| `edit_style` | string | `anchor` | the patch format; dalgona defaults to `hashline` |\n| `guard` | table | `{ enabled = false }` | guard settings; dalgona defaults to `enabled = true`; see [Guard](guard.md) |\n| `search_symbols` | bool | `false` | whether search includes symbols; dalgona defaults to `true` |\n| `judge` | table | `none` | shared judge settings, including `gate`; readiness checks do not generate a response; see [Judged rules](rules.md#judged-rules) |\n| `plugins` | list | `[]` | the `plugins` setting |\n| `aliases` | table | `none` | the `aliases` setting |\n| `serve` | table | `none` | local control endpoint settings; field defaults apply when omitted |\n| `serve.bind` | string | `127.0.0.1` | the `serve.bind` setting |\n| `serve.port` | number | `7437` | the `serve.port` setting |\n| `serve.token_file` | path | `\"<data-root>/serve.token\"` | the authentication token file |\n| `serve.approval` | string | `ask` | the `serve.approval` setting |\n| `serve.origins` | list | `[]` | the `serve.origins` setting |\n| `prices` | table | `none` | the `prices` setting |\n| `prices.<model-id>.input` | number | `none` | the `prices.<model-id>.input` setting |\n| `prices.<model-id>.cached_input` | number | `none` | the `prices.<model-id>.cached_input` setting |\n| `prices.<model-id>.output` | number | `none` | the `prices.<model-id>.output` setting |\n| `prices.<model-id>.reasoning` | number | `none` | the `prices.<model-id>.reasoning` setting |\n| `rules` | table | `none` | rule matching and retry settings; field defaults apply when omitted |\n| `rules.watch` | bool | `true` | whether to watch stream rules |\n| `rules.interrupt` | string | `always` | interrupt mode when a rule does not set its own |\n| `rules.repeat` | string | `once` | repeat mode when a rule does not set its own |\n| `rules.repeat_gap` | number | `10` | turn gap for `after-gap` repeats |\n| `rules.max_retries` | number | `3` | maximum rule interrupts per turn |\n| `rules.disabled` | list | `[]` | the `rules.disabled` setting |\n| `rules.judge` | string | `auto` | `off` excludes judged rules; `auto` includes them only when the judge is ready; `on` requires readiness at session startup; separate from `judge.gate`; see [Judged rules](rules.md#judged-rules) |\n| `sandbox_writable` | list | `[]` | the `sandbox_writable` setting |\n| `agents` | table | `none` | the `agents` setting |\n| `limits` | table | `none` | the `limits` setting |\n| `models` | table | `none` | the `models` setting |\n| `retry` | table | `none` | the `retry` setting |\n| `providers` | table | `none` | the `providers` setting |\n| `ask` | table | `none` | the `ask` setting |\n| `ask.timeout` | string | `\"30m\"` | how long one raised question stays open; clamped into `1m` to `120m` at load, with one startup notice naming the key, the value, and the clamp |\n| `compact` | table | `none` | the `compact` setting |\n| `rule_sets` | table | `none` | the `rule_sets` setting |\n| `plugin` | table | `none` | the `plugin` setting |\n| `eval` | table | `none` | the `eval` setting |\n| `eval.uses` | list | `[]` | the operations that eval cells may request, such as `tools.read`, `tools.search`, `tools.patch`, `tools.exec`, or an exported `tools.<plugin>.<tool>`; each effect still goes through normal approval, with at most 64 entries and no wildcards or duplicates; empty means eval cells can only compute and cannot act |\n\n`[aliases]` maps model aliases. `[serve]` holds router settings. `[limits]` holds run budgets. `[plugin.<name>]` holds per-plugin settings.\n\n```toml\nplugins = [\"todo\"]\n```\n\nEvery config error names the key and the fix.\n"),
    ("convert-pi", "# Port a pi extension to a dal plugin\n\n## Before you start\n\nA pi extension is TypeScript that pi loads with jiti; a dal plugin is Starlark that dal evaluates at load. Both run with your permissions; neither is a sandbox. A port is a redesign more often than a translation. Read the `plugins` page first: it defines every construct named below.\n\n## Get the source\n\nFrom npm, `npm pack <package>` prints a file name, then `tar -xzf <file>.tgz` unpacks it into `./package`. From git, `git clone <url> package`. Pi's own examples live in `packages/coding-agent/examples/extensions/` of the pi repository.\n\n## Take inventory\n\n```sh\ngrep -RnoE '\\b(pi|ctx)\\.[A-Za-z_$][A-Za-z0-9_$]*' package --include='*.ts'\ngrep -RhoE \"from '[^.][^']*'\" package --include='*.ts' | sort -u\n```\n\n## See one tool ported\n\nA pi tool of this shape:\n\n```ts\npi.registerTool({\n  name: \"greet\",\n  description: \"Greet someone by name.\",\n  parameters: Type.Object({ name: Type.String() }),\n  async execute(id, params) {\n    return { content: [{ type: \"text\", text: `Hello, ${params.name}.` }] };\n  },\n});\n```\n\nbecomes this plugin, saved as `greet/plugin.star`:\n\n```starlark\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef greet(ctx, args):\n    return \"Hello, \" + args.name + \".\"\n\ngreet_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"Greet someone by name.\",\n    input = dal.schema(name = dal.string()),\n    run = greet,\n)\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"greet\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    tools = {\"greet\": greet_tool},\n)\n```\n\nThe tool name moves from `name` to its key in `tools`. The schema moves from `parameters` to `input`. The result moves from a content list to the returned value.\n\n## Map each call\n\n| pi | dal | Shown in |\n|---|---|---|\n| `export default function (pi: ExtensionAPI)` | `plugin = dal.plugin(name = \"...\", version = \"...\")` in `plugin.star`, after one `load(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")`; `name` matches the directory | every port |\n| `pi.registerTool({name, description, parameters, execute})` | `dal.tool(description, input, run, uses)`, published as `tools = {\"<name>\": tool}`; `uses` lists the operations the handler may request | todo, subagent |\n| TypeBox `Type.Object`, string enums | `dal.schema(...)` with `dal.string`, `dal.integer`, `dal.enum([...])`, `dal.list`; `dal.optional` for a field a caller may omit | todo, ask |\n| `execute(id, params, signal, onUpdate, ctx)` | `run(ctx, args)`; dal cancels the call and discards the result; progress text is not ported | subagent |\n| result `{content, details}` | the returned value; `dal.output(value = ..., view = ...)` when people should see a table or text apart from the data; `details` has no counterpart | todo (the returned value) |\n| `throw` inside `execute` | `return dal.err(code, message)` for a failure the model should read; a failed operation stops the handler by itself | fetch |\n| `renderCall`, `renderResult` | the result and its `view`; plugin UI is data | todo |\n| `pi.registerCommand(name, {handler})` | `dal.command(tool = tool, positional = [...])` under `commands`; the command runs a tool and is typed as `/<plugin>:<key>` | todo, git-checkpoint |\n| `ctx.ui.select`, `confirm`, `input` | `ctx.ask.select`, `ctx.ask.confirm`, `ctx.ask.text`, with the `ask.*` ids in `uses` | ask |\n| `pi.exec(cmd, args, {cwd, timeout})` | `ctx.tools.exec(\"cmd args\")` with `tools.exec` in `uses`; the approval ladder applies; a non-zero exit stops the handler unless you call `ctx.try_call(ctx.tools.exec, \"...\")`; `ctx.describe(\"tools.exec\")` lists the other keywords | git-checkpoint |\n| `fetch`, npm HTTP clients | `ctx.net.fetch(...)` with `net.fetch` in `uses` | fetch |\n| `pi.sendUserMessage(text)` | `ctx.turn.steer(...)` with `turn.steer` in `uses`; `ctx.turn.wake` starts a turn when dal is idle | steer |\n| state in `details`, `pi.appendEntry` | `ctx.state.read(key = ...)` and `ctx.state.write(key = ..., value = ..., expected = revision)`, with `state.read` and `state.write` in `uses` | todo, plan-mode, handoff |\n| `before_agent_start` adds to `systemPrompt` | `prompt = \"...\"` on the plugin for fixed text; a `before_turn` hook that returns `event.append(text)` for per-turn text; the hook cannot read state | hello (`prompt`) |\n| `pi.on(\"tool_call\")` | `dal.on(\"tool_call\", handler)`; the handler returns `event.allow()`, `event.block(reason)`, or `event.rewrite(args)` | permission-gate |\n| other `pi.on` events | `dal.on` with `input`, `before_turn`, `before_request`, `tool_result`, `turn_end`, `session_start`, `session_end`, or `settled`; each event fixes its verdict methods and the operations it may request | none |\n| `pi.on(\"user_bash\")`, whole-operation swap | does not port; no hook can hand over execution | (not-port table) |\n| `pi.registerProvider` | does not port; providers are dal core and aliases live in config | (not-port table) |\n| `ctx.ui.custom`, widgets, status, editor | does not port; plugin UI is data | (not-port table) |\n| `ctx.modelRegistry` nested model call | `ctx.models.infer` with `models.infer` in `uses`, or a child session through `ctx.agents.start` and `ctx.agents.wait` | subagent, handoff (child sessions) |\n| `pi.registerFlag`, `registerShortcut` settings | `config = dal.schema(...)` on the plugin and a `[plugin.<name>]` table in `dal.toml`; the handler reads `ctx.config` | permission-gate, mcp |\n| `pi.events` plugin bus | does not port; a handler cannot call another plugin's tool | (not-port table) |\n| stdio MCP servers | `ctx.mcp.call` with `mcp.call` in `uses`, where dal has an MCP client (dalgona ships one); an external bridge program elsewhere | mcp |\n| project `.pi/extensions` | never loads; plugins come from the data root only | (not-port table) |\n| TypeScript or npm at run time | an external program through `ctx.tools.exec`, or a port of the logic | (not-port table) |\n| `resources_discover` dynamic skills | `skills = {\"<name>\": dal.skill(description = ..., path = \"skills/<name>/SKILL.md\")}`, fixed at load; no dynamic discovery | skill-pack |\n\n## Decide\n\nIf the purpose of the extension is in the not-port table, do not port it.\n\n| pi shape | why it does not port | what dal or dalgona offers instead |\n|---|---|---|\n| `interactive-shell` | needs user takeover of the terminal; `tools.exec` waits for the child to exit and captures its streams; `ask` is one modal question | none today; the door can widen |\n| `status-line` | needs a persistent status or footer surface; plugin UI is data, and the only widget is `ask` | none today; the door can widen |\n| `pi.on(\"user_bash\")` swap | no hook can hand over execution | run the program as a tool through `ctx.tools.exec` |\n| `pi.registerProvider` | providers are dal core | config aliases |\n| `ctx.ui.custom`, widgets, status, editor | plugin UI is data | the ask surface |\n| `pi.events` bus | a handler cannot call another plugin's tool | one plugin, or a file that both read |\n| project `.pi/extensions` | plugins come from the data root only | install into the data root |\n| TypeScript or npm at run time | nothing runs TypeScript at load | an external program through `ctx.tools.exec`, or a port of the logic |\n| web fetch, MCP client as a product | dalgona's batteries own them | dalgona's `web` and `mcp` batteries; in dal the `net.fetch` operation |\n\n## Create the plugin directory\n\n```sh\nd=\"${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/dal/plugins/<name>\"; mkdir -p \"$d\"\n```\n\nOn Windows the data root follows the `install` page.\n\n## Start from the nearest example\n\nA tool with state, `todo`; child sessions, `subagent`; questions to the user, `ask`; an MCP call, `mcp`; a hook, `permission-gate`; skills only, `skill-pack`; the smallest shape, `hello`; an HTTP fetch, `fetch`; turn steering, `steer`; review rules, `review-rules`; file search and read, `locate`; session hooks with state, `session-log`; one environment variable, `env-read`. The page `dal://examples/<name>` holds every file; from the repository root of a source checkout, run `cp -r examples/plugins/<name>/. \"$d/\"`. Then set `name` and rename the tools and commands.\n\n## Translate\n\nTranslate one registration at a time with the map. The worked pair: pi `examples/extensions/todo.ts:105-297` beside the `todo` handler of `examples/plugins/todo/plugin.star`.\n\n## Turn it on\n\nAdd the name to the plugins key in `${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/dal/dal.toml`:\n\n```toml\nplugins = [\"<name>\"]\n```\n\nRun dal. A load error stops startup and names `path:line:col`. Then run one print-mode turn that uses the new tool, with the CLI print-mode flag.\n\n## Change and reload\n\nEdit, then type `/reload`. Success prints counts, for example `Reloaded 2 plugins: 3 tools. New turns use them. A running turn keeps the plugins it started with.` Failure prints `reload failed: <rendered error>` on one line and `the previous plugin set stays live` on the next. With no plugins configured it prints `No plugins to reload: the plugins key in dal.toml is empty.`\n\n## Check it in dalgona\n\nCopy the directory to the dalgona data root, add the name to dalgona's config, and run `dalgona`. A plugin that loads in dalgon loads in dalgona.\n\n## Write README.md\n\nWrite `README.md` in the plugin directory with sections `# <name>`, `Ported from`, `What it shows`, `What differs from pi`, `Install`, `Settings`, at most 60 lines. When you translate pi code, keep pi's MIT notice; `dal://examples` holds the text.\n\n## Let dal port it\n\nType: `Port the pi extension in ./package to a dal plugin named <name>. Read dal://convert-pi and follow its steps. Write the plugin under the dal data directory, and tell me what did not port.` The model writes into the data root, so the approval ladder asks first. The `port-pi-extension` skill of `skill-pack` routes the model here.\n"),
    ("examples", "# Example plugins for dal\n\nPorts of pi extensions as dal Starlark plugins, plus original dal plugins with no pi counterpart. Each port keeps its behavior; pi-specific UI becomes data.\n\n| port | pi origin | what it shows |\n|---|---|---|\n| hello | pi `examples/extensions/hello.ts` | the smallest shape |\n| todo | pi `todo.ts` | a tool with state |\n| subagent | pi `subagent/index.ts` | bounded child sessions |\n| ask | pi `questionnaire.ts` | questions to the user |\n| mcp | the shape of pi-mcp-adapter | MCP calls through `mcp.call` and plugin settings |\n| permission-gate | pi `permission-gate.ts` | a `tool_call` hook and settings |\n| plan-mode | the shape of pi-plan-mode | a command that toggles saved state |\n| git-checkpoint | pi `git-checkpoint.ts` | listing and restoring checkpoints through `tools.exec` |\n| handoff | pi `handoff.ts` | child session notes saved to state |\n| skill-pack | pi `dynamic-resources` | skills only |\n| fetch | original to dal | HTTP fetch through `net.fetch`, with `dal.err` failures |\n| steer | original to dal | steering or waking the turn through `turn.steer` and `turn.wake` |\n| review-rules | original to dal | stream and always-apply rules through `dal.rule` |\n| locate | original to dal | file search and read through `tools.search` and `tools.read` |\n| session-log | original to dal | `session_start` and `turn_end` hooks with state |\n| env-read | original to dal | one allowed variable through `env.read` and plugin settings |\n\n## pi's license\n\nThis product includes Starlark translations of examples of pi (https://github.com/earendil-works/pi), Copyright (c) 2025 Mario Zechner, used under the MIT License.\n\nPermission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of this software and associated documentation files (the \"Software\"), to deal in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:\n\nThe above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all copies or substantial portions of the Software.\n\nTHE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED \"AS IS\", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM, OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE SOFTWARE.\n"),
    ("examples/ask", "# ask: Ask the user several questions in a row.\n\n## Ported from\npi `questionnaire`\n\n## What it shows\nQuestions to the user through `ask.select` and `ask.text`. The `questionnaire` tool takes 1 to 8 questions, each with an `id`, a `prompt`, and optional `options` (2 to 10 strings). A question with options is a choice list with one extra choice, \"Type an answer\". A question without options is a text prompt. The tool returns one `id: answer` line per question, and a blank answer reads `no answer`. The `ask` operations need to be declared in `uses` but need no grant.\n\nWithout a connected controller that can answer questions, each question returns\n`no answer`. The tool does not wait for input or choose an option for you.\n\n## What differs from pi\nEach question is one modal prompt, asked in order. There is no form or custom screen. The schema accepts an `allow_other` field, but the handler does not read it.\n\n## Install\nCopy to the dal plugins data root and add `ask` to `plugins`.\n\n## Settings\nNone.\n\n## Files\n\n### plugin.star\n\n```starlark\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef questionnaire(ctx, args):\n    lines = []\n    for question in args.questions:\n        qid = question.id\n        prompt = question.prompt\n        options = getattr(question, \"options\", dal.MISSING)\n        if options == dal.MISSING:\n            options = []\n        if options:\n            choices = [{\"label\": option} for option in options]\n            choices.append({\"label\": \"Type an answer\"})\n            answer = ctx.ask.select(prompt = prompt, options = choices, multi = False)\n        else:\n            answer = ctx.ask.text(prompt = prompt)\n        if not answer:\n            answer = \"no answer\"\n        lines.append(qid + \": \" + answer)\n    return \"\\n\".join(lines)\n\nquestion = dal.schema(\n    id = dal.string(),\n    prompt = dal.string(),\n    options = dal.optional(dal.list(dal.string(), min_len = 2, max_len = 10)),\n    allow_other = dal.optional(dal.boolean()),\n)\n\nquestionnaire_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"Ask the user several questions in a row and return every answer. Use it when you need more than one decision before you continue.\",\n    input = dal.schema(questions = dal.list(question, min_len = 1, max_len = 8)),\n    uses = [\"ask.select\", \"ask.text\"],\n    run = questionnaire,\n)\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"ask\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    tools = {\"questionnaire\": questionnaire_tool},\n)\n```\n"),
    ("examples/env-read", "# env-read: Read one environment variable.\n\n## Ported from\nOriginal to dal; no pi counterpart.\n\n## What it shows\nOne tool over `env.read` and a plugin setting. The `read` tool takes a variable name, or falls back to the `key` setting from `[plugin.env-read]`. The host reads from the environment it captured at start. An unset variable returns an `env-read` error that names it. With no name and no `key` setting, the tool returns an error that says how to give one.\n\n## What differs from pi\nPi reads `process.env` directly. Here the first read asks for the plugin's `env` grant, and the tool declares exactly one operation.\n\n## Install\nCopy to the dal plugins data root and add `env-read` to `plugins`.\n\n## Settings\n`key`: the default variable name when the tool gets none.\n\n## Files\n\n### plugin.star\n\n```starlark\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef read_env(ctx, args):\n    name = args.name\n    if not name:\n        name = ctx.config.key\n    if not name:\n        return dal.err(\"env-read\", \"give a name, or set key in [plugin.env-read]\")\n    value = ctx.env.read(key = name)\n    if value == None:\n        return dal.err(\"env-read\", name + \" is not set\")\n    return value\n\nread_env_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"Read one environment variable. Give a name, or set a default key in [plugin.env-read].\",\n    input = dal.schema(name = dal.string(default = \"\")),\n    uses = [\"env.read\"],\n    run = read_env,\n)\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"env-read\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    config = dal.schema(key = dal.string(default = \"\")),\n    tools = {\"read\": read_env_tool},\n    commands = {\n        \"env\": dal.command(\n            tool = read_env_tool,\n            positional = [\"name\"],\n            description = \"Read an environment variable.\",\n        ),\n    },\n)\n```\n"),
    ("examples/fetch", "# fetch: Fetch one URL over HTTP.\n\n## Ported from\nOriginal to dal; no pi counterpart. Pi reaches the network with `fetch` or an npm HTTP client.\n\n## What it shows\nThe `net.fetch` operation and a failure the model can read. The `fetch` tool takes a `url`, sends an HTTP GET through `net.fetch`, and returns one line with the status and the body size. A transport failure returns a `fetch_failed` error naming the URL and the host message; a non-200 status returns a `fetch_failed` error naming the status. The command `/fetch:fetch` takes the URL as its positional argument.\n\n## What differs from pi\nPi code awaits a response object and reads its body as text. This plugin reads `status` and the length of `body` from the host response record and never renders the body itself.\n\n## Install\nCopy to the dal plugins data root and add `fetch` to `plugins`.\n\n## Settings\nNone.\n\n## Files\n\n### plugin.star\n\n```starlark\n\"\"\"Fetch one URL over HTTP and report its status and body size.\"\"\"\n\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef fetch_url(ctx, args):\n    \"\"\"Fetch one URL and return its status line.\n\n    Args:\n        ctx: Invocation context with the net service.\n        args: Command arguments holding the url to fetch.\n\n    Returns:\n        One line with the status and body size, or a fetch_failed\n        domain error when the request or the status fails.\n    \"\"\"\n    result = ctx.try_call(ctx.net.fetch, method = \"GET\", url = args.url)\n    if not result.ok:\n        return dal.err(\"fetch_failed\", \"Could not fetch \" + args.url + \": \" + result.error.message)\n    response = result.value\n    if response.status != 200:\n        return dal.err(\"fetch_failed\", \"GET \" + args.url + \" returned status \" + str(response.status))\n    return \"GET \" + args.url + \": status 200, \" + str(len(response.body)) + \" body bytes\"\n\nfetch_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"Fetch one URL with an HTTP GET and report its status and body size.\",\n    input = dal.schema(url = dal.string()),\n    uses = [\"net.fetch\"],\n    run = fetch_url,\n)\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"fetch\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    tools = {\"fetch\": fetch_tool},\n    commands = {\"fetch\": dal.command(tool = fetch_tool, positional = [\"url\"], description = \"Fetch one URL.\")},\n)\n```\n"),
    ("examples/git-checkpoint", "# git-checkpoint: List and restore git stash checkpoints.\n\n## Ported from\npi `git-checkpoint`\n\n## What it shows\nA command that runs a program through `tools.exec`. The command `/git-checkpoint:checkpoint` accepts positional arguments `action` (`list` or `restore`, default `list`) and `n` (default `0`). `list` runs `git stash list` and prints the result, or `no checkpoints` when it is empty. `restore` runs `git stash apply stash@{n}`. Every run passes the approval ladder, so dal asks before it runs a command.\n\n## What differs from pi\nThis plugin only lists and restores. It does not create checkpoints. The pi hook runs `git stash create` at each turn start and offers to apply that stash when you branch from the turn. A failed git command stops the handler with its error.\n\n## Install\nCopy to the dal plugins data root and add `git-checkpoint` to `plugins`.\n\n## Settings\nNone.\n\n## Files\n\n### plugin.star\n\n```starlark\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef checkpoint(ctx, args):\n    if args.action == \"restore\":\n        return ctx.tools.exec(command = \"git stash apply stash@{\" + str(args.n) + \"}\")\n    result = ctx.tools.exec(command = \"git stash list\")\n    if not result:\n        return \"no checkpoints\"\n    return result\n\ncheckpoint_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"List or restore checkpoints.\",\n    input = dal.schema(\n        action = dal.enum([\"list\", \"restore\"], default = \"list\"),\n        n = dal.integer(min = 0, default = 0),\n    ),\n    uses = [\"tools.exec\"],\n    run = checkpoint,\n)\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"git-checkpoint\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    commands = {\n        \"checkpoint\": dal.command(\n            tool = checkpoint_tool,\n            positional = [\"action\", \"n\"],\n            description = \"List checkpoints.\",\n        ),\n    },\n)\n```\n"),
    ("examples/handoff", "# handoff: Distill the session into a handoff note.\n\n## Ported from\npi `handoff`\n\n## What it shows\nChild sessions, saved state, and tool execution together. The command `/handoff:handoff` has no positional parameters. Pass `--note=...`, or give the model tool `handoff` its optional `note` argument. The child receives a prompt for the goal, state, next steps, and open questions. Its default session name is `handoff`; the host refuses to start it if that name is already used in the workspace.\n\nAfter the child finishes, the plugin saves its report under the session state key `handoff.md`. Success returns `Handoff written to handoff.md. Start the next session and ask for it.` Handoff requires durable session state. In an ephemeral session, the host refuses state access with `denied: state is unavailable`; no note is saved.\n\n## What differs from pi\nThis plugin does not switch the parent session. It stores the note in the plugin's saved state. Child progress text is not shown. Cancelling a parent turn during `agents.wait` closes the awaited child and its descendants. Before the wait begins, cancel started children explicitly rather than relying on turn cancellation. Closing the parent session closes its descendant sessions.\n\n## Install\nCopy to the dal plugins data root and add `handoff` to `plugins`.\n\n## Settings\nNone.\n\n## Files\n\n### plugin.star\n\n```starlark\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef handoff(ctx, args):\n    note = args.note\n    started = ctx.agents.start(\n        prompt = \"Distill this session into a handoff note for a fresh session: current goal, state, next steps, open questions. \" + note,\n    )\n    report = ctx.agents.wait(id = started.value.id)\n    text = report.value.report.text\n    state = ctx.try_call(ctx.state.read, key = \"handoff.md\")\n    if not state.ok:\n        if state.error.code == \"unavailable\":\n            return \"handoff: no sidecar in an ephemeral session\"\n        return state.unwrap()\n    saved = ctx.try_call(\n        ctx.state.write,\n        key = \"handoff.md\",\n        value = text,\n        expected = state.value.revision,\n    )\n    if not saved.ok:\n        if saved.error.code == \"unavailable\":\n            return \"handoff: no sidecar in an ephemeral session\"\n        return saved.unwrap()\n    return \"Handoff written to handoff.md. Start the next session and ask for it.\"\n\nhandoff_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"Write a handoff note.\",\n    input = dal.schema(note = dal.string(default = \"\")),\n    uses = [\"agents.start\", \"agents.wait\", \"state.read\", \"state.write\"],\n    run = handoff,\n)\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"handoff\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    commands = {\"handoff\": dal.command(tool = handoff_tool, description = \"Write a handoff note.\")},\n)\n```\n"),
    ("examples/hello", "# hello: The smallest dal plugin.\n\n## Ported from\npi `hello`\n\n## What it shows\nOne tool, one prompt section, and one skill in a single `plugin` value. The `hello` tool takes no arguments and returns a greeting. The prompt section tells the model to greet the user warmly. The `hello` skill points at `skills/hello/SKILL.md`.\n\n## What differs from pi\nNothing to translate: pi's `hello` registers one tool, and so does this plugin. The prompt section and the skill are additions that show the other two kinds of registration.\n\n## Install\nCopy to the dal plugins data root and add `hello` to `plugins`.\n\n## Settings\nNone.\n\n## Files\n\n### plugin.star\n\n```starlark\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef hello(ctx, _args):\n    return \"Hello from a dal plugin.\"\n\nhello_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"Say hello.\",\n    input = dal.schema(),\n    run = hello,\n)\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"hello\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    tools = {\"hello\": hello_tool},\n    prompt = \"Greet the user warmly.\",\n    skills = {\n        \"hello\": dal.skill(\n            description = \"The smallest dal plugin: one tool, one prompt section, one skill.\",\n            path = \"skills/hello/SKILL.md\",\n        ),\n    },\n)\n```\n\n### skills/hello/SKILL.md\n\n```markdown\n# hello\n\nThe smallest skill.\n```\n"),
    ("examples/locate", "# locate: Search the workspace or read a file.\n\n## Ported from\nOriginal to dal; no pi counterpart.\n\n## What it shows\nOne tool over two file operations. The `lookup` tool reads the file named by `path`; it returns the file text and passes the read view to `dal.output` for display. Without a path, it searches the workspace for `pattern` and returns the matches and the `truncated` flag. With neither, it returns a `locate` error. The command `/locate:locate` takes the pattern as its positional argument.\n\n## What differs from pi\nPi gives the model separate read and search tools. Here one tool picks the operation from its arguments.\n\n## Install\nCopy to the dal plugins data root and add `locate` to `plugins`.\n\n## Settings\nNone.\n\n## Files\n\n### plugin.star\n\n```starlark\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef lookup(ctx, args):\n    if args.path:\n        view = ctx.tools.read(args.path)\n        text = \"\\n\".join([row.text for row in view.rows])\n        return dal.output(value = text, view = view)\n    if not args.pattern:\n        return dal.err(\"locate\", \"give a pattern to search for, or a path to read\")\n    page = ctx.tools.search(args.pattern)\n    matches = [{\"path\": hit.path, \"line\": hit.line, \"text\": hit.text} for hit in page.matches]\n    return {\"matches\": matches, \"truncated\": page.truncated}\n\nlookup_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"Find text in the workspace or read one file. Give a pattern to search for, or a path to read.\",\n    input = dal.schema(\n        pattern = dal.string(default = \"\"),\n        path = dal.string(default = \"\"),\n    ),\n    uses = [\"tools.read\", \"tools.search\"],\n    run = lookup,\n)\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"locate\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    tools = {\"lookup\": lookup_tool},\n    commands = {\n        \"locate\": dal.command(\n            tool = lookup_tool,\n            positional = [\"pattern\"],\n            description = \"Search the workspace or read a file.\",\n        ),\n    },\n)\n```\n"),
    ("examples/mcp", "# mcp: List and call MCP tools.\n\n## Ported from\nthe shape of pi-mcp-adapter\n\n## What it shows\nPlugin settings and the `mcp.call` operation. Both tools declare `uses = [\"mcp.call\"]`:\n\n- `mcp_list`: prints the servers named in the `servers_json` setting, formatted as `name  url` lines.\n- `mcp_call`: takes `server`, `tool`, and `arguments_json` (holding a JSON object string, default `{}`). It decodes the arguments, calls the remote tool with `mcp.call`, and returns the response text.\n\nIf an MCP response reports an error (`isError = true`), both tools return a failed tool outcome under the `mcp_error` error code with the remote message.\n\n## What differs from pi\nThis plugin starts no MCP server and holds no direct connection. `mcp_list` reads configured servers from the setting. Pure dal does not bundle an MCP client backend, so calls fail closed as unavailable unless a client implementation is composed into the host; dalgona provides an MCP battery. stdio servers require an external bridge or battery.\n\n## Install\nCopy to the dal plugins data root and add `mcp` to `plugins`.\n\n## Settings\n`servers_json` is optional text holding a JSON object with a `servers` map:\n\n```toml\n[plugin.mcp]\nservers_json = '{\"servers\": {\"docs\": {\"url\": \"https://example.com/mcp\"}}}'\n```\n\n## Files\n\n### plugin.star\n\n```starlark\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef mcp_settings(ctx):\n    raw = getattr(ctx.config, \"servers_json\", dal.MISSING)\n    if raw == dal.MISSING or not raw:\n        return {\"servers\": {}, \"timeout_ms\": 30000}\n    return json.decode(raw)\n\ndef mcp_list(ctx, args):\n    servers = mcp_settings(ctx).get(\"servers\", {})\n    server = args.server\n    if server:\n        info = servers.get(server, {})\n        if not info:\n            return \"\"\n        response = ctx.mcp.call(server = server, tool = \"\", arguments = {})\n        if getattr(response, \"isError\", False):\n            return dal.err(\"mcp_error\", response.text)\n        if not response.text:\n            return server + \"  \" + info.get(\"url\", \"\")\n        return server + \"  \" + info.get(\"url\", \"\") + \"\\n\" + response.text\n    lines = []\n    for name in sorted(servers.keys()):\n        lines.append(name + \"  \" + servers[name].get(\"url\", \"\"))\n    return \"\\n\".join(lines)\n\ndef mcp_call(ctx, args):\n    arguments = json.decode(args.arguments_json)\n    response = ctx.mcp.call(server = args.server, tool = args.tool, arguments = arguments)\n    if getattr(response, \"isError\", False):\n        return dal.err(\"mcp_error\", response.text)\n    return response.text\n\nmcp_list_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"List configured MCP servers.\",\n    input = dal.schema(server = dal.string(default = \"\")),\n    uses = [\"mcp.call\"],\n    run = mcp_list,\n)\n\nmcp_call_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"Call one MCP tool with arguments_json containing a JSON object.\",\n    input = dal.schema(\n        server = dal.string(default = \"\"),\n        tool = dal.string(default = \"\"),\n        arguments_json = dal.string(default = \"{}\"),\n    ),\n    uses = [\"mcp.call\"],\n    run = mcp_call,\n)\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"mcp\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    config = dal.schema(servers_json = dal.optional(dal.string())),\n    tools = {\"mcp_list\": mcp_list_tool, \"mcp_call\": mcp_call_tool},\n)\n```\n"),
    ("examples/permission-gate", "# permission-gate: Block risky tool calls by pattern.\n\n## Ported from\npi `permission-gate`\n\n## What it shows\nA `tool_call` hook and plugin settings. The hook turns the call's arguments into text and blocks the call when the text contains `rm -rf`, `sudo`, or `force`, or a pattern you add. The block reason names the tool, the arguments, and the matching pattern. Any other call is allowed. The command `/permission-gate:gate` reports how many patterns are active.\n\n## What differs from pi\nA `tool_call` hook decides on its own: it can allow, block, or rewrite the call. It cannot ask you a question, because a deciding hook may request no operations. The match is a plain substring test, so it is a guard rail, not a security boundary.\n\n## Install\nCopy to the dal plugins data root and add `permission-gate` to `plugins`.\n\n## Settings\n`patterns` is an optional list of extra strings to block:\n\n```toml\n[plugin.permission-gate]\npatterns = [\"drop table\"]\n```\n\n## Files\n\n### plugin.star\n\n```starlark\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef load_patterns(ctx):\n    patterns = [\"rm -rf\", \"sudo\", \"force\"]\n    extra = getattr(ctx.config, \"patterns\", dal.MISSING)\n    if extra != dal.MISSING:\n        patterns = patterns + [pattern for pattern in extra]\n    return patterns\n\ndef on_tool_call(ctx, event):\n    tool = str(event.tool)\n    summary = str(event.args)\n    for pattern in load_patterns(ctx):\n        if pattern and pattern in summary:\n            return event.block(reason = 'permission-gate: blocked ' + tool + ' ' + summary + ' (matches \"' + pattern + '\")')\n    return event.allow()\n\ndef gate(ctx, _args):\n    return \"permission-gate: \" + str(len(load_patterns(ctx))) + \" patterns active\"\n\ngate_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"Show active patterns.\",\n    input = dal.schema(),\n    run = gate,\n)\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"permission-gate\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    config = dal.schema(patterns = dal.optional(dal.list(dal.string()))),\n    commands = {\"gate\": dal.command(tool = gate_tool, description = \"Show active patterns.\")},\n    hooks = [dal.on(\"tool_call\", on_tool_call)],\n)\n```\n"),
    ("examples/plan-mode", "# plan-mode: Toggle a plan-mode flag.\n\n## Ported from\nthe shape of pi-plan-mode\n\n## What it shows\nA command that flips saved state. `/plan-mode:plan` reads the `plan-mode` key, writes `on` or `off` with the revision it read, and prints `plan mode: on` or `plan mode: off`.\n\n## What differs from pi\nThe flag is only recorded in session state. No hook reads it, because `before_turn` and `tool_call` hooks cannot read state under current plugin capabilities. As a result, this plugin does not block edits and does not inject plan guidance into the prompt. In an ephemeral session, state operations fail closed as unavailable.\n\n## Install\nCopy to the dal plugins data root and add `plan-mode` to `plugins`.\n\n## Settings\nNone.\n\n## Files\n\n### plugin.star\n\n```starlark\n\"\"\"Toggle a persisted plan-mode setting with the /plan command.\"\"\"\n\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef plan(ctx, _args):\n    \"\"\"Toggle plan mode and return its new state.\n\n    Args:\n        ctx: Invocation context with access to plugin state.\n        _args: Unused command arguments.\n\n    Returns:\n        A message that states whether plan mode is on or off.\n    \"\"\"\n    record = ctx.state.read(key = \"plan-mode\")\n    if record.present and record.value == \"on\":\n        ctx.state.write(key = \"plan-mode\", value = \"off\", expected = record.revision)\n        return \"plan mode: off\"\n    ctx.state.write(key = \"plan-mode\", value = \"on\", expected = record.revision)\n    return \"plan mode: on\"\n\nplan_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"Toggle plan mode.\",\n    input = dal.schema(),\n    uses = [\"state.read\", \"state.write\"],\n    run = plan,\n)\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"plan-mode\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    commands = {\"plan\": dal.command(tool = plan_tool)},\n)\n```\n"),
    ("examples/review-rules", "# review-rules: Stream and always-apply rules for review.\n\n## Ported from\nOriginal to dal; no pi counterpart. Pi has no rule surface; dalgona documents project rules separately.\n\n## What it shows\nThe `dal.rule` constructor in both shapes. The `no-todo` rule watches model output for the `TODO` pattern and answers with its text on a match. The `always-test` rule sets `always_apply = True` and applies every turn with no pattern. The prompt section adds one fixed review instruction to the system prompt.\n\n## What differs from pi\nThere is nothing to translate. A rule needs a pattern unless it always applies; both forms live side by side in one `rules` map.\n\n## Install\nCopy to the dal plugins data root and add `review-rules` to `plugins`.\n\n## Settings\nNone.\n\n## Files\n\n### plugin.star\n\n```starlark\n\"\"\"Review rules: one stream rule and one always-apply rule.\"\"\"\n\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"review-rules\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    prompt = \"Review every code change for tests and error handling.\",\n    rules = {\n        \"no-todo\": dal.rule(\n            pattern = \"TODO\",\n            text = \"Do not leave TODO markers in new code; file a tracked task instead.\",\n        ),\n        \"always-test\": dal.rule(\n            text = \"Run the affected package tests before reporting a change complete.\",\n            always_apply = True,\n        ),\n    },\n)\n```\n"),
    ("examples/session-log", "# session-log: Remember sessions and turns in state.\n\n## Ported from\nOriginal to dal; no pi counterpart.\n\n## What it shows\nTwo stateful hooks plus a reader. The `session_start` hook records `start` and the `turn_end` hook records `turn` in the `session-log.json` state key. Both hooks call through `ctx.try_call`, so an ephemeral session records nothing and raises nothing. The list keeps the newest 50 entries. The `log` tool and the `/session-log:log` command print one marker per line.\n\n`session_start` runs before a client attaches, so no one can answer a grant question then. The first open of a session records no `start`. If you approve the plugin's state grant for good rather than for one session, later opens record `start`.\n\n## What differs from pi\nPi keeps this kind of journal in extension memory. Here the hooks persist it in plugin state, so a reopened session reads what earlier turns wrote.\n\n## Install\nCopy to the dal plugins data root and add `session-log` to `plugins`.\n\n## Settings\nNone. State needs a saved session; an ephemeral session records nothing.\n\n## Files\n\n### plugin.star\n\n```starlark\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef remember(ctx, mark):\n    seen = ctx.try_call(ctx.state.read, key = \"session-log.json\")\n    if not seen.ok:\n        if seen.error.code == \"unavailable\":\n            return None\n        seen.unwrap()\n    marks = []\n    if seen.value.present:\n        marks = [entry for entry in seen.value.value]\n    marks.append(mark)\n    if len(marks) > 50:\n        marks = marks[len(marks) - 50:]\n    saved = ctx.try_call(\n        ctx.state.write,\n        key = \"session-log.json\",\n        value = marks,\n        expected = seen.value.revision,\n    )\n    if not saved.ok:\n        if saved.error.code == \"unavailable\":\n            return None\n        saved.unwrap()\n    return None\n\ndef on_start(ctx, _event):\n    remember(ctx, \"start\")\n    return None\n\ndef on_turn_end(ctx, _event):\n    remember(ctx, \"turn\")\n    return None\n\ndef show_log(ctx, _args):\n    record = ctx.state.read(key = \"session-log.json\")\n    if not record.present:\n        return \"session-log: no turns recorded yet\"\n    lines = []\n    for entry in record.value:\n        lines.append(\"- \" + entry)\n    return \"\\n\".join(lines)\n\nlog_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"Show the session markers recorded by the session hooks.\",\n    input = dal.schema(),\n    uses = [\"state.read\"],\n    run = show_log,\n)\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"session-log\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    tools = {\"log\": log_tool},\n    commands = {\"log\": dal.command(tool = log_tool, description = \"Show recorded session markers.\")},\n    hooks = [\n        dal.on(\"session_start\", on_start, uses = [\"state.read\", \"state.write\"]),\n        dal.on(\"turn_end\", on_turn_end, uses = [\"state.read\", \"state.write\"]),\n    ],\n)\n```\n"),
    ("examples/skill-pack", "# skill-pack: Skills that port and write plugins.\n\n## Ported from\npi `dynamic-resources`\n\n## What it shows\nA plugin that publishes only skills. `port-pi-extension` routes the model to `dal://convert-pi` when you name a pi extension. `write-plugin` routes it to `dal://plugins` and `dal://examples` when you ask for a new tool, command, or skill. Each skill body is a short file under `skills/`, read when the plugin loads.\n\n## What differs from pi\nSkills are fixed at load. pi's `dynamic-resources` discovers resources while it runs; a plugin cannot.\n\n## Install\nCopy to the dal plugins data root and add `skill-pack` to `plugins`.\n\n## Settings\nNone.\n\n## Files\n\n### plugin.star\n\n```starlark\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"skill-pack\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    skills = {\n        \"port-pi-extension\": dal.skill(\n            description = \"Port a pi extension (TypeScript) to a dal plugin (Starlark). Use when the user names a pi extension, a pi package on npm, or a .ts file that calls pi.registerTool or pi on.\",\n            path = \"skills/port-pi-extension/SKILL.md\",\n        ),\n        \"write-plugin\": dal.skill(\n            description = \"Write a new dal plugin. Use when the user asks for a new tool, slash command, or skill.\",\n            path = \"skills/write-plugin/SKILL.md\",\n        ),\n    },\n)\n```\n\n### skills/port-pi-extension/SKILL.md\n\n```markdown\n# port-pi-extension\n\nRead `dal://convert-pi` whole and follow its steps in order.\n```\n\n### skills/write-plugin/SKILL.md\n\n```markdown\n# write-plugin\n\nRead `dal://plugins` and `dal://examples`, and start from the nearest example.\n```\n"),
    ("examples/steer", "# steer: Steer the running turn or wake an idle one.\n\n## Ported from\nOriginal to dal; no pi counterpart. Pi nudges the session with `pi.sendUserMessage(text)`.\n\n## What it shows\nThe `steer` tool takes `text` and checks `ctx.turn.is_idle().value`.\nThe `is_idle` reply is a tagged record; its `value` field holds the Boolean.\nThe tool calls `turn.wake` for an idle turn and `turn.steer` for a running turn.\nIt returns a message that states which path it took.\nThe command `/steer:steer` takes the text as its positional argument.\n\n## What differs from pi\nPi sends a user message into the session unconditionally. This plugin branches on idleness first, because steering an idle turn and waking a running one are different requests to the host.\n\n## Install\nCopy to the dal plugins data root and add `steer` to `plugins`.\n\n## Settings\nNone.\n\n## Files\n\n### plugin.star\n\n```starlark\n\"\"\"Steer the running turn, or wake an idle turn with steering text.\"\"\"\n\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef steer_turn(ctx, args):\n    \"\"\"Hand steering text to the turn.\n\n    Args:\n        ctx: Invocation context with the turn service.\n        args: Command arguments holding the steering text.\n\n    Returns:\n        A message that states whether the text steered the running\n        turn or woke an idle one.\n    \"\"\"\n    if ctx.turn.is_idle().value:\n        ctx.turn.wake(text = args.text)\n        return \"woke an idle turn: \" + args.text\n    ctx.turn.steer(text = args.text)\n    return \"steered the running turn: \" + args.text\n\nsteer_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"Add steering text to the running turn, or wake an idle turn with it.\",\n    input = dal.schema(text = dal.string(min_len = 1)),\n    uses = [\"turn.is_idle\", \"turn.steer\", \"turn.wake\"],\n    run = steer_turn,\n)\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"steer\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    tools = {\"steer\": steer_tool},\n    commands = {\"steer\": dal.command(tool = steer_tool, positional = [\"text\"], description = \"Steer the turn.\")},\n)\n```\n"),
    ("examples/subagent", "# subagent: Run tasks in separate dal child sessions.\n\n## Ported from\npi `subagent`\n\n## What it shows\nChild sessions through `agents.start`, `agents.wait`, and `agents.cancel`. The `subagent` tool takes a single `task` string, or a `tasks` list of 1 to 8 items. If neither is provided, it returns `no task`.\n\nSingle-task execution:\n1. Starts one child session with `agents.start`, passing the task as the prompt. The child runs in the same workspace with a fresh context.\n2. Awaits the child with `agents.wait` to collect its final report.\n3. Closes the completed child with `agents.cancel`.\n4. Returns the report text. If the host refuses to start the child (for example, when maximum child depth is reached), the tool returns `subagent: child refused: <reason>`.\n\nMulti-task batches:\nThe handler attempts to start children in batches of up to 4 concurrent sessions before awaiting them. However, `start_child` supplies no explicit session name, so every child inherits the default plugin name (`subagent`). Because session names must be unique within a workspace, starting a second concurrent child is rejected by the host with a duplicate name error. The tool cleans up earlier started children in the batch with `agents.cancel` and returns the start error.\n\n## What differs from pi\nThe child receives only prompt text; its progress is not streamed back. A tool cannot call another plugin's tool, so delegation uses a child session. Cancelling a parent turn during `agents.wait` closes the awaited child and its descendants. Before the wait begins, cancel started children explicitly rather than relying on turn cancellation. Closing the parent session closes its descendant sessions.\n\n## Install\nCopy to the dal plugins data root and add `subagent` to `plugins`. Subagents are off by default in dal, so a session must allow them before the tool can start a child.\n\n## Settings\nNone.\n\n## Files\n\n### plugin.star\n\n```starlark\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef cancel_children(ctx, handles):\n    error = \"\"\n    for handle in handles:\n        result = ctx.try_call(ctx.agents.cancel, id = handle)\n        if not result.ok and not error:\n            error = \"subagent: child cancel failed: \" + result.error.message\n    return error\n\ndef start_child(ctx, task):\n    result = ctx.try_call(\n        ctx.agents.start,\n        prompt = task,\n    )\n    if not result.ok:\n        return None, \"subagent: child start failed: \" + result.error.message\n    started = result.value\n    if started.type != \"started\":\n        return None, \"subagent: child refused: \" + started.value.reason.type\n    return started.value.id, \"\"\n\ndef wait_child(ctx, handle):\n    result = ctx.try_call(ctx.agents.wait, id = handle)\n    if not result.ok:\n        return None, \"subagent: child wait failed: \" + result.error.message\n    report = result.value\n    if report.type != \"await\":\n        return None, \"subagent: child did not finish: \" + report.type\n    return report.value.report.text, \"\"\n\ndef run_batch(ctx, tasks):\n    handles = []\n    for task in tasks:\n        handle, error = start_child(ctx, task)\n        if error:\n            cleanup_error = cancel_children(ctx, handles)\n            if cleanup_error:\n                return [], cleanup_error\n            return [], error\n        handles.append(handle)\n    answers = []\n    for handle in handles:\n        answer, error = wait_child(ctx, handle)\n        if error:\n            cleanup_error = cancel_children(ctx, handles)\n            if cleanup_error:\n                return [], cleanup_error\n            return [], error\n        answers.append(answer)\n        cleanup_error = cancel_children(ctx, [handle])\n        if cleanup_error:\n            cancel_children(ctx, handles)\n            return [], cleanup_error\n    return answers, \"\"\n\ndef subagent(ctx, args):\n    tasks = getattr(args, \"tasks\", dal.MISSING)\n    if tasks == dal.MISSING:\n        tasks = []\n    if not tasks:\n        single = args.task\n        if single:\n            tasks = [single]\n    if not tasks:\n        return \"no task\"\n    answers = []\n    batch = []\n    for task in tasks:\n        batch.append(task)\n        if len(batch) == 4:\n            batch_answers, error = run_batch(ctx, batch)\n            if error:\n                return error\n            for answer in batch_answers:\n                answers.append(answer)\n            batch = []\n    if batch:\n        batch_answers, error = run_batch(ctx, batch)\n        if error:\n            return error\n        for answer in batch_answers:\n            answers.append(answer)\n    return \"\\n\".join(answers)\n\nsubagent_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"Run a task in a separate dal child session with a fresh context and return its final answer. Give one task, or up to 8 tasks that run 4 at a time. The child works in the same workspace.\",\n    input = dal.schema(\n        task = dal.string(default = \"\"),\n        tasks = dal.optional(dal.list(dal.string(), min_len = 1, max_len = 8)),\n    ),\n    uses = [\"agents.start\", \"agents.wait\", \"agents.cancel\"],\n    run = subagent,\n)\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"subagent\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    tools = {\"subagent\": subagent_tool},\n)\n```\n"),
    ("examples/todo", "# todo: Keep a todo list for this task.\n\n## Ported from\npi `todo`\n\n## What it shows\nOne tool with saved state. The `todo` tool takes an action (`list`, `add`, `toggle`, or `clear`), plus `text` for `add` and `id` for `toggle`:\n\n- `list`: prints each item as `[ ] #<id>: <text>` or `[x] #<id>: <text>`, or `No todos` when the list is empty.\n- `add`: requires non-empty `text`. Assigns an ID one greater than the highest existing ID and returns `Added todo #<id>: <text>`. Omitting `text` returns an error: `todo: text is required for add`.\n- `toggle`: requires a nonzero `id`. Flips `done` and returns `Todo #<id> completed` or `Todo #<id> uncompleted`. Omitting `id` returns `todo: id is required for toggle`; an unknown ID returns `todo: #<id> not found`.\n- `clear`: empties the list and returns `Cleared <n> todos`.\n\nThe list lives in session state under the key `todos`. Each write passes the revision from the read before it, so a stale write fails with a revision conflict instead of overwriting. The command `/todo:todos` prints the list.\n\n## What differs from pi\npi keeps the list in the `details` of tool results and rebuilds it from the session. This plugin keeps it in saved state through `state.read` and `state.write`. In an ephemeral session, state operations fail closed as unavailable because no persistent store exists. There is no custom rendering: the tool returns plain text.\n\n## Install\nCopy to the dal plugins data root and add `todo` to `plugins`.\n\n## Settings\nNone.\n\n## Files\n\n### plugin.star\n\n```starlark\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef todo_load(ctx):\n    record = ctx.state.read(key = \"todos\")\n    items = []\n    if record.present:\n        for item in record.value:\n            items.append({\"id\": item.id, \"text\": item.text, \"done\": item.done})\n    return items, record.revision\n\ndef todo_save(ctx, items, revision):\n    ctx.state.write(key = \"todos\", value = items, expected = revision)\n\ndef todo(ctx, args):\n    action = args.action\n    if action == \"list\":\n        items, _revision = todo_load(ctx)\n        if not items:\n            return \"No todos\"\n        lines = []\n        for item in items:\n            mark = \"x\" if item[\"done\"] else \" \"\n            lines.append(\"[\" + mark + \"] #\" + str(item[\"id\"]) + \": \" + item[\"text\"])\n        return \"\\n\".join(lines)\n    if action == \"add\":\n        text = args.text\n        if not text:\n            return dal.err(\"todo\", \"text is required for add\")\n        items, revision = todo_load(ctx)\n        new_id = 1\n        for item in items:\n            if item[\"id\"] >= new_id:\n                new_id = item[\"id\"] + 1\n        items.append({\"id\": new_id, \"text\": text, \"done\": False})\n        todo_save(ctx, items, revision)\n        return \"Added todo #\" + str(new_id) + \": \" + text\n    if action == \"toggle\":\n        target = args.id\n        if not target:\n            return dal.err(\"todo\", \"id is required for toggle\")\n        items, revision = todo_load(ctx)\n        for item in items:\n            if item[\"id\"] == target:\n                item[\"done\"] = not item[\"done\"]\n                todo_save(ctx, items, revision)\n                if item[\"done\"]:\n                    return \"Todo #\" + str(target) + \" completed\"\n                return \"Todo #\" + str(target) + \" uncompleted\"\n        return dal.err(\"todo\", \"#\" + str(target) + \" not found\")\n    if action == \"clear\":\n        items, revision = todo_load(ctx)\n        count = len(items)\n        todo_save(ctx, [], revision)\n        return \"Cleared \" + str(count) + \" todos\"\n    return \"No todos\"\n\ntodo_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"Keep a todo list for this task. Actions: list, add (text), toggle (id), clear.\",\n    input = dal.schema(\n        action = dal.enum([\"list\", \"add\", \"toggle\", \"clear\"], default = \"list\"),\n        text = dal.string(default = \"\"),\n        id = dal.integer(default = 0),\n    ),\n    uses = [\"state.read\", \"state.write\"],\n    run = todo,\n)\n\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"todo\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    tools = {\"todo\": todo_tool},\n    commands = {\"todos\": dal.command(tool = todo_tool, description = \"Print the todo list.\")},\n)\n```\n"),
    ("guard", "# The complexity guard\n\nFour report groups: turn growth, per-file absolute metrics, per-function metric changes, best current. The guard is off by default in dal and on in dalgona. Notices name the file and the metric. To turn it off, set `enabled` in the `[guard]` table in `dal.toml`:\n\n```toml\n[guard]\nenabled = false\n```\n"),
    ("install", "# Install dal and dalgona\n\nBinaries: `dalgon` (primary, with `dal` and `dl` as aliases) and `dalgona` (with `dg` as alias).\n\nBuild from source. No tagged release exists, so the registry and installer channels have nothing to fetch yet.\n\n```sh\ngit clone https://github.com/gosuda/dalgona\ncd dalgona\nrustup update stable --no-self-update\ncargo build --release -p dalgon\ncargo build --release -p dalgona\n```\n\nThe binaries land in `target/release/`. When the first `dalgon-v*` and `dalgona-v*` tags land, the registry and installer channels serve the same binaries.\n\nSource builds use the latest stable Rust toolchain. Run\n`rustup update stable --no-self-update` before building. The repository uses one\nroot Cargo workspace and one `Cargo.lock`; it does not declare a minimum\nsupported Rust version.\n\nPlatforms: Linux, macOS, Windows on x86_64 and aarch64. Building from source needs a C toolchain because the symbols grammars compile through `cc`; prebuilt release binaries arrive with the first tagged release. If another package already installed `dal`, `dl`, or `dalgon`, Cargo refuses to overwrite without `--force`. Windows binaries are unsigned in v0 and may trigger SmartScreen; verify the release checksum before running them.\n\nConfig and data roots per OS follow [Settings in dal.toml](config.md). Plugins need no toolchain.\n"),
    ("integrate", "# Build a client on the dal protocol\n\nChoose a way in. GUI client, step by step: spawn `dalgon rpc`, send `initialize`, call `session/list`, `session/open`, `session/subscribe`, render updates, answer requests exactly once, cancel when needed, read blobs with `blob/read`, reconnect with `gen` and `after`. Generate types from `protocol/schema`. Editors connect through ACP.\n\nRules for clients:\n\n- R1: answer each request exactly once.\n- R2: never invent missing updates; resync on gaps.\n- R3: cancel cleanly; do not abandon turns.\n- R4: keep tokens secret; never log them.\n- R5: one adapter per transport; no adapter stacks on another.\n- R6: validate server replies tolerantly; ignore unknown fields.\n"),
    ("models", "# Providers, models, and thinking levels\n\nFour API families: OpenAI Chat Completions, OpenAI Responses, OpenAI Codex with the ChatGPT sign-in, Anthropic. Run `dalgon login`, then pick a model. First run lists models; aliases live in config. Thinking levels map per API and clamp to model limits; temperature requires thinking to be off. Once delivered semantic output begins, in-stream transport cuts do not retry; pre-delivery transport cuts retry. Luna Reserve holds prepaid balance. Codex sends `originator: dalgon`; Claude shapes requests byte for byte as the reference harness does.\n"),
    ("philosophy", "# Philosophy: one door\n\n# dal\n\ndal is a small coding agent with one door. Everything that extends it comes in through that door: plugins, other programs, every way in, and dalgona's batteries. The primary binary is `dalgon`, with `dal` and `dl` as aliases.\n\n## Why dal\n\nNo private inside; its own batteries register through the plugin interface. dal alone is a complete agent; dalgona adds opinions; both load the same plugins.\n\n## One door\n\nIn process, the Rust interface of the Host and Agent seam carries five operations over one snapshot, one change stream, one command path, one request and answer pair, one blob read; the TUI uses these and nothing else. Across processes, a JSON-RPC protocol carries the same operations, served by `dalgon rpc` over stdio or a local socket, with a JSON Schema for every message; `docs/read` serves both manuals.\n\n## Plugins in Starlark\n\nA plugin is a directory with one `plugin.star` in the data root. A plugin publishes one `plugin` value that registers tools, commands, hooks, skills, rules, and prompt text, and it declares the operations each tool and hook may request; every effect still needs your approval. Nine lifecycle events exist, each with a declared result type; a Rust extension sees a tenth. Questions travel as data to whichever way in you use. Load is free of compile steps. A broken plugin stops startup and names `path:line:col`. `/reload` re-evaluates and prints counts (for example `Reloaded 2 plugins: 3 tools. New turns use them. A running turn keeps the plugins it started with.`); on failure it prints `reload failed: <rendered error>` and `the previous plugin set stays live`. A plugin that loads in dalgon loads in dalgona. A plugin runs with your permissions, so read it before you install it.\n\n## Models\n\nFour API families exist. Run `dalgon login`, then pick a model. `/model` and `/thinking` change them in a session.\n\n## History you can trust\n\nEach session is a journal; a record counts as saved only after it is synced to disk. A session is a tree; `-c` continues, `-r` resumes; `/export` writes a file you can share yourself.\n\n## Context you control\n\nThe system prompt is built from parts you control (`AGENTS.md` files, `SYSTEM.md`, skills, rules, plugin sections); `dal://prompt` lists them. Compaction starts by default at 85 percent of the context window. Long, stable text can go out as an image and the exact text stays readable at `letter://<id>`.\n\n## Tools and approval\n\nNormal mode gives the model `read`, `search`, `patch`, and `exec`; eval-first adds `eval`; eval-only leaves only `eval` visible. A call you deny outside `eval` is denied inside it, and `eval` is not a sandbox. `patch` speaks several edit styles, one setting picks the style, and the style follows the model. By default dal asks before every `patch` and every `exec`. `--approval ask|edits|all` carries the ladder. When no person can answer, dal denies and says why. An optional sandbox, off by default, guards the home directory.\n\n## Steer or follow up\n\nYou can type while dal works. A steer reaches the model at the next safe point. A follow-up waits for the turn to end. Stop ends the turn at once.\n\n## Many ways in, one protocol\n\n| Way in | Start |\n|---|---|\n| Terminal (inline) | `dal` |\n| Terminal (fullscreen) | `dal` with fullscreen screen |\n| Print | `dalgon` print flags |\n| JSON | `dalgon` JSON flags |\n| ACP | editor ACP adapter |\n| RPC | `dalgon rpc` |\n| Router | `dalgon serve` |\n| A2A | `dalgon serve --a2a` |\n\n`dalgon serve` listens on loopback only; `--public` requires a token for remote clients arriving through a trusted HTTPS/WSS reverse proxy on the same host. No desktop, web, or mobile app ships; any such app is a client of the protocol, not a fork.\n\n## Built for large runs\n\nThe stress gate exercises 501 child sessions and 201 shell jobs with scripted models. On the idle-machine timing lane (`DAL_TIMING_BUDGETS`), it requires RSS below 256 MiB, fewer than 4096 open handles, and cancellation P99 below 250 ms. These are gate limits, not promises about your machine.\n\n## Text in every language\n\ndal measures text the way your terminal draws it; the cursor, wrapping, and cuts never split a character.\n\n## The manual is inside\n\ndal documents itself at `dal://`; dalgona adds `dalgona://`; the model reads pages with `read`; you read them with `dalgon docs`.\n\n## dalgona\n\ndalgona is dal with batteries, and every built-in battery is enabled by default through dal's same public extension door. The built-in batteries are orchestration, history, quality, judged, web, work, MCP, review, ask, skills, and ttsr-rules. By default, dalgona enables symbol search, AST edit, the `hashline` edit style, and the complexity guard; it also provides plan and todo, rich ask, web search and fetch, an MCP client, and a review loop. It keeps its own configuration, data, and sessions. This repository uses one root Cargo workspace and one root `Cargo.lock` to resolve dalgona's dal dependencies locally; releases publish dal and dalgona separately. dal and dalgona are distributed under the Sustainable Use License; the licensor is `metaphorics`.\n\n## What we did not build\n\nNo MCP client in dal (dalgona ships one; a plugin can reach HTTP services with the `net.fetch` operation). No plugins from the workspace. No sandbox by default and no network restriction. No plan or todo in dal (dalgona ships both). No subagents in dal by default (the `agents` operations are one door away). No LSP, ever, in either product (search plus AST edit is the surface). No upload (`/export` writes a file you can share yourself). No self-rebuild (`/reload` reloads plugins). Nothing but Starlark in `eval`. No second store (the journal is the only store). No browser front end in the binary.\n"),
    ("plugins", "# Plugins: tools, commands, hooks, skills, and rules\n\nA plugin is Starlark that adds tools, slash commands, hooks, skills, rules, and prompt text to dal. It is one directory holding one file, `plugin.star`, and that file publishes one value named `plugin`. Nothing else registers: a tool you build but do not attach to `plugin` does not exist. A plugin runs with your permissions, so read it before you install it.\n\n## Write a first plugin\n\nMake a directory under the data root. Its name is the plugin name.\n\n```sh\nd=\"${XDG_DATA_HOME:-$HOME/.local/share}/dal/plugins/quality\"; mkdir -p \"$d\"\n```\n\nOn Windows the data root follows the `install` page. Save this as `plugin.star` in that directory:\n\n```starlark\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef find_todos(ctx, args):\n    page = ctx.tools.search(\"TODO\", path = args.path)\n    return {\n        \"matches\": [\n            {\"path\": hit.path, \"line\": hit.line, \"text\": hit.text}\n            for hit in page.matches[:args.limit]\n        ],\n        \"truncated\": page.truncated or len(page.matches) > args.limit,\n    }\n\ntodos = dal.tool(\n    description = \"Find TODO comments under a path.\",\n    input = dal.schema(\n        path = dal.string(default = \".\"),\n        limit = dal.integer(default = 20, min = 1, max = 200),\n    ),\n    uses = [\"tools.search\"],\n    run = find_todos,\n)\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"quality\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    tools = {\"todos\": todos},\n    commands = {\n        \"todos\": dal.command(tool = todos, positional = [\"path\"]),\n    },\n)\n```\n\nTurn it on by adding its name to the `plugins` key in `${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/dal/dal.toml`:\n\n```toml\nplugins = [\"quality\"]\n```\n\nRun dal. The model can now call the tool, and you can type `/quality:todos src`.\n\nFour things carry the whole design:\n\n- `load(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")` appears once. It is the only import a plugin may use.\n- A handler is `run(ctx, args)`. dal checks the arguments against `input`, fills defaults, and only then calls it.\n- `uses` lists every operation the handler may request. A call to any other operation is denied and ends the handler.\n- `plugin` is the only value published. Its `name` must equal the directory name.\n\n## Fill in the plugin value\n\n`dal.plugin` takes keywords only. `name` and `version` are required.\n\n| keyword | value | default |\n|---|---|---|\n| `name` | the directory name: lowercase letters, digits, `_`, `-`, starting with a letter, at most 64 characters | required |\n| `version` | a SemVer string such as `\"0.1.0\"` | required |\n| `config` | a `dal.schema` for the plugin's settings | none |\n| `state_version` | a positive integer naming the shape of saved state | `1` |\n| `tools` | a map from a local key to a `dal.tool` | empty |\n| `commands` | a map from a key to a `dal.command` | empty |\n| `hooks` | a list of `dal.on` | empty |\n| `skills` | a map from a name to a `dal.skill` | empty |\n| `rules` | a map from a name to a `dal.rule` | empty |\n| `prompt` | one static prompt section, or `None` | `None` |\n| `models` | a map from a local key to a `dal.model` | empty |\n\nA tool key `todos` in plugin `quality` has the identity `quality.todos`. One `dal.tool` can sit under at most one key. A tool with no key still serves a command. Saved state is kept apart for each plugin and each `state_version`; changing the version does not migrate old state.\n\n## Define a tool\n\n`dal.tool` takes keywords only.\n\n| keyword | meaning | default |\n|---|---|---|\n| `description` | what the model reads to choose the tool | required |\n| `input` | a `dal.schema` for the arguments | required |\n| `run` | the handler, `run(ctx, args)` | required |\n| `uses` | the operations the handler may request | `[]` |\n| `output` | a `dal.schema` the returned value must satisfy | none |\n| `visibility` | `\"model\"`, `\"deferred\"`, or `\"eval_only\"` | `\"model\"` |\n\n`\"model\"` puts the tool in the model's ordinary tool list. `\"deferred\"` keeps it out of the list until the model first calls it by name; the call runs at once and the tool joins the list from the next request on. A request offers exactly one way to find deferred tools. On first-party Anthropic models from Opus, Sonnet, Fable, and Mythos 4.5 on, Anthropic's own tool search finds them, and each rides the request marked `defer_loading`. Every other model gets the `tool_search` tool, which lists matches and changes nothing. A plugin tool named `tool_search` replaces both. `\"eval_only\"` reaches the tool only through `eval`.\n\n### Describe arguments with a schema\n\n`dal.schema(**fields)` builds the schema. Each field is one of these constructors. Keywords are named; the first argument of `enum`, `list`, `optional`, and `nullable` is positional.\n\n| constructor | accepts |\n|---|---|\n| `dal.string` | `min_len`, `max_len`, `default` |\n| `dal.integer` | `min`, `max`, `default` |\n| `dal.number` | `min`, `max`, `default` |\n| `dal.boolean` | `default` |\n| `dal.enum(values)` | a list of strings, `default` |\n| `dal.list(item)` | `min_len`, `max_len`; `item` is a bare type |\n| `dal.optional(type)` | the caller may leave the field out |\n| `dal.nullable(type)` | the field is required but may be `None` |\n\nA field is required unless it has a `default` or is wrapped by `optional`. When a caller leaves out an `optional` field, the handler reads `dal.MISSING`:\n\n```starlark\ndef note(ctx, args):\n    if args.tag == dal.MISSING:\n        return \"No tag given.\"\n    return \"Tag: \" + args.tag\n```\n\nField names are plain identifiers and never start with `_`. Bounds must not contradict each other. Nothing is coerced: the string `\"5\"` does not satisfy an integer field, and a truthy value does not satisfy a boolean.\n\n### Return a result\n\nReturn plain data and it is the result. Three helpers say more:\n\n- `dal.ok(value)` returns a success explicitly.\n- `dal.err(code, message, details)` returns a failure the model can read. `details` is optional. The codes `failed`, `exit_nonzero`, `unavailable`, `conflict`, `busy`, `cancelled`, `observation_unavailable`, `invocation_mismatch`, and `indeterminate` belong to dal; `dal.err` refuses them.\n- `dal.output(value = ..., view = ...)` keeps the data and its display apart. A view is `{\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"...\"}`, `{\"type\": \"table\", \"columns\": [...], \"rows\": [[...]]}`, an intact view from `tools.read`, or a list of views. Table rows hold scalar values. A view changes what people see, never the status of the call.\n\n```starlark\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef count(ctx, args):\n    page = ctx.tools.search(args.pattern, path = args.path)\n    total = len(page.matches)\n    return dal.output(\n        value = {\"count\": total, \"truncated\": page.truncated},\n        view = {\n            \"type\": \"table\",\n            \"columns\": [\"pattern\", \"matches\"],\n            \"rows\": [[args.pattern, total]],\n        },\n    )\n\ndef branch(ctx, args):\n    result = ctx.try_call(ctx.tools.exec, \"git branch --show-current\")\n    if not result.ok:\n        return dal.err(\"no_git\", \"Could not read the branch: \" + result.error.message)\n    return dal.ok(result.value)\n\ncount_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"Count matches for a pattern.\",\n    input = dal.schema(\n        pattern = dal.string(min_len = 1),\n        path = dal.string(default = \".\"),\n    ),\n    uses = [\"tools.search\"],\n    run = count,\n)\n\nbranch_tool = dal.tool(\n    description = \"Show the current git branch.\",\n    input = dal.schema(),\n    uses = [\"tools.exec\"],\n    run = branch,\n)\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"report\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    tools = {\"count\": count_tool, \"branch\": branch_tool},\n)\n```\n\nA plain dictionary that happens to contain `ok = False` is still data. Only `dal.err` reports a failure.\n\n## Reach the outside with ctx\n\n`ctx.config` holds the validated settings. Every other field of `ctx` is a group of operations, and each call returns its value directly:\n\n```starlark\nview = ctx.tools.read(\"src/lib.rs\")\n```\n\n`tools.read` returns a view with `path`, `header`, `truncated`, and `rows`; each row has `line`, `text`, and `complete`. `tools.search` returns a page with `matches` and `truncated`; each match has `path`, `line`, `text`, and a `source` read view. A handler cannot return a view itself: return plain data, and pass the view as the `view` of `dal.output` to display it.\n\nAn operation that fails stops the handler with the error. To handle an expected failure yourself, pass the operation, not its result, to `ctx.try_call`. You get back a result with `ok`, `value`, and `error`; `unwrap()` returns the value or raises the failure again. Denial, cancellation, and hard limits still stop the handler; `try_call` cannot catch them and never retries.\n\nThese are the operations. A handler may call only those in its `uses`.\n\n| group | operations | purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| `tools` | `read`, `search`, `patch`, `exec` | read files, search, edit files, run a command |\n| `models` | `infer` | ask a model for a completion |\n| `net` | `fetch` | make an HTTP request to an allowed destination |\n| `ask` | `confirm`, `select`, `text` | ask the user a question |\n| `state` | `read`, `write`, `delete` | keep data for this plugin and session |\n| `agents` | `start`, `wait`, `cancel`, `list` | run child sessions |\n| `jobs` | `start`, `wait`, `cancel`, `list`, `text` | run background jobs |\n| `turn` | `cancel`, `steer`, `wake`, `is_idle` | steer the running turn or start one |\n| `env` | `read` | read an allowed environment variable |\n| `mcp` | `call` | call an MCP tool when a client is present |\n\nWrite the id as `group.operation`, for example `\"state.write\"`. Ids are exact: no wildcards, no duplicates, at most 64 per declaration. `tools.read` takes `path` as its positional argument, `tools.search` takes `pattern`, and `tools.exec` takes `command`. Every other argument is named. Call `ctx.describe(\"tools.exec\")` to see the keywords of one operation.\n\n`agents.start` does not accept a per-call `tools` list; passing one fails with an argument error. Omitting `name` defaults the child session name to the plugin name; because session names are unique per workspace, concurrent child sessions require distinct explicit names. It answers `refused` with a typed `reason` when policy or input stops a child: `max_depth` (`child sessions cannot start children here: agents.max_depth = <n>.`), `workspace_unresolved`, `workspace_outside_root`, or `model_unroutable`. Show the reason text to the user; a `cancelled` reply still means the child was cancelled.\n\n`uses` asks; it does not grant. Each effect still needs a live grant and passes the approval ladder, so `tools.patch` and `tools.exec` ask before they run. `ask` operations need to be in `uses` but need no grant. `turn.wake` refuses after 20 turns in a row that wake started with no user prompt. `models.forward` exists only for model handlers, so plugins cannot use it.\n\nState is explicit and session-scoped. A read returns a record with `present`, `value` (when present), and `revision`. A write or delete must pass the `revision` from a read as `expected`. A stale revision fails with `conflict`, and `ctx.try_call` can catch that. Keys match `[a-z][a-z0-9_.-]{0,63}`. An ephemeral session returns `unavailable` for state. The `todo` example keeps a list this way; read it at `dal://examples/todo`.\n\nA handler cannot call another plugin's tool. One plugin can share code between its handlers with plain functions, and two plugins can read the same file.\n\n## Add a command\n\n`dal.command(tool = ..., positional = [...], description = ...)` binds a slash command to a tool. The command runs that tool once, with the same schema and defaults a model call gets. Without `description`, the tool's description is the command's summary. The command name is `/<plugin>:<key>`; the key uses lowercase letters, digits, `_`, and `-`, starts with a letter, and has at most 64 characters. A name that clashes with a built-in command is an error that names both claimants.\n\n`positional` lists scalar fields in order. A required field cannot follow an optional one. The rest take `--field=value`, or `--field value` for strings. Booleans take `--field` and `--no-field`. Lists and records take one JSON value: `--field=JSON`. `--` ends option parsing. Single and double quotes group a token, and a backslash escapes the next character outside single quotes. Assigning one field twice is an error. Nothing expands: no globs, variables, or shell.\n\n## Hook into the turn\n\n`dal.on(event, run, uses = [...])` runs `run(ctx, event)` at one lifecycle event. The event value carries its payload as attributes, such as `event.tool`, and the methods that return a verdict.\n\n| event | payload | the handler returns | may request |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `session_start` | `session`, `workspace`, `resumed` | `None` | `state.read`, `state.write`, `state.delete` |\n| `session_end` | `session`, `reason` | `None` | `state.read`, `state.write`, `state.delete` |\n| `input` | `text` | `event.continue_()`, `event.transform(text)`, `event.handled()` | nothing |\n| `before_turn` | `turn`, `text` | `event.continue_()`, `event.append(text)` | nothing |\n| `before_request` | `turn`, `round`, `model`, `caps`, `params` | `event.continue_()`, `event.params(...)` | nothing |\n| `tool_call` | `turn`, `call`, `tool`, `args` | `event.allow()`, `event.block(reason)`, `event.rewrite(args)` | nothing |\n| `tool_result` | `turn`, `call`, `tool`, `ok`, `preview` | `None` | `state.read`, `state.write`, `state.delete` |\n| `turn_end` | `turn`, `stop` | `None` | `state.read`, `state.write`, `state.delete` |\n| `settled` | `turn` | `None` | `state.read` |\n\nThe four hooks that decide something (`input`, `before_turn`, `before_request`, `tool_call`) are pure: they see only the event, the plugin's config, and fixed metadata, and they may declare no `uses`. Naming an operation outside a hook's column is a load error. `event.params` takes `thinking`, `effort`, `temperature`, and `max_output_tokens`; dal clamps them to the model's limits. `preview` holds at most 4 KiB of the result.\n\nA verdict is valid only for the event that made it. Returning `None`, a dictionary, or another event's verdict from a deciding hook is a failure, not an implicit allow. A failed `tool_call` hook blocks the call; a failed `input`, `before_turn`, or `before_request` hook changes nothing. A failed observer is recorded and leaves the result unchanged.\n\nHooks run in one fixed order: built-ins, then bundled plugins by name, then your plugins by name; inside one plugin, in list order. A `rewrite` feeds the next hook. The first `block` or `handled` ends the chain, except that on `input` a built-in `handled` still lets later origins run. `before_turn` texts join with a blank line.\n\n```starlark\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\ndef guard_patch(ctx, event):\n    if event.tool == \"patch\" and ctx.config.read_only:\n        return event.block(\"Patch is disabled by this plugin's policy.\")\n    return event.allow()\n\ndef remember(ctx, event):\n    record = ctx.state.read(key = \"last_tool\")\n    ctx.state.write(key = \"last_tool\", value = event.tool, expected = record.revision)\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"policy\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    config = dal.schema(read_only = dal.boolean(default = False)),\n    hooks = [\n        dal.on(\"tool_call\", guard_patch),\n        dal.on(\"tool_result\", remember, uses = [\"state.read\", \"state.write\"]),\n    ],\n)\n```\n\nThis hook blocks `patch` by name. It does not stop `exec` from writing a file. A hook is policy, not a boundary; the boundary is the approval ladder and the sandbox.\n\n## Read settings\n\n`config = dal.schema(...)` declares the settings. Values come from the `[plugin.<name>]` table in `dal.toml`:\n\n```toml\n[plugin.policy]\nread_only = true\n```\n\nThe handler reads `ctx.config.read_only`. An unknown key, a wrong type, or an invalid default fails at load, before any handler runs. Read an optional setting with `getattr(ctx.config, \"name\", dal.MISSING)`. A plugin gets settings only this way.\n\n## Add skills, rules, and prompt text\n\n`dal.skill(description, path)` publishes a skill. The path is relative to the plugin directory, so `skills/review/SKILL.md` is read when the plugin loads. `letter2image = True` opts the skill into letter handling, which the `prompt` page describes. Skills collide by name: the first claimant wins, and an ambiguous short name refuses to resolve.\n\n`dal.rule(text, pattern, judge, always_apply, scope, interrupt_mode, repeat_mode, repeat_gap)` publishes a rule; every argument is a keyword and only `text` is required. A rule with a `pattern`, a regular expression, watches the model's output; on a match, `text` is what the model is told. A rule with `always_apply = True` and no `pattern` applies every turn. A rule needs a pattern unless it always applies; a rule with neither fails at the call. `judge` is optional instruction text for judged rules.\n\nThe last four arguments narrow where and how often a rule fires. Leave one out to keep the `[rules]` default.\n\n| Argument | Values |\n| --- | --- |\n| `scope` | a list of `text`, `thinking`, `tool`, or `tool:<name>`, such as `[\"tool:exec\"]` |\n| `interrupt_mode` | `always`, `prose-only`, `tool-only`, or `never` |\n| `repeat_mode` | `once` or `after-gap` |\n| `repeat_gap` | a whole number from 1 to 1000 |\n\nA value outside these lists stops the plugin from loading, and the error names the argument and the values to use. Rules, the `[rules]` settings, and the judged-rule gate are on the `rules` page.\n\n`prompt` is one fixed section of the system prompt.\n\n```starlark\nload(\"@dal/v1\", \"dal\")\n\nplugin = dal.plugin(\n    name = \"style\",\n    version = \"0.1.0\",\n    prompt = \"Prefer small, reviewable changes.\",\n    skills = {\n        \"review\": dal.skill(\n            description = \"Review a diff for missing tests. Use when the user asks for a review.\",\n            path = \"skills/review/SKILL.md\",\n        ),\n    },\n    rules = {\n        \"no-todo\": dal.rule(\n            pattern = \"\\\\bTODO\\\\b\",\n            text = \"Finish the TODO now, or say why it stays.\",\n            scope = [\"text\", \"tool:patch\"],\n            repeat_mode = \"after-gap\",\n            repeat_gap = 10,\n        ),\n        \"small-diffs\": dal.rule(\n            text = \"Keep each change small enough to review at a glance.\",\n            always_apply = True,\n        ),\n    },\n)\n```\n\n## Call a plugin tool from eval\n\nAn `eval` cell can call a published tool as `tools[\"<plugin>.<key>\"]`. The cell needs both the tool's id and the operations that tool declares:\n\n```starlark\ntools[\"quality.todos\"](path = \"src\")\n```\n\nThat cell needs `tools.quality.todos` and `tools.search` in its operations. The `tools` page explains how a cell gets them.\n\n## Load, reload, and errors\n\nLoad has no compile step. A broken plugin stops startup and names `path:line:col`. Load is all or nothing: one bad plugin stops the whole set. Type `/reload` to re-evaluate; it prints counts, for example `Reloaded 2 plugins: 3 tools. New turns use them. A running turn keeps the plugins it started with.` On failure it prints `reload failed: <rendered error>` on one line and `the previous plugin set stays live` on the next.\n\nPlugins come from the data root only, never from the workspace. A plugin that loads in dalgon loads in dalgona; see `dalgona://batteries` for the batteries product.\n\n## Declare scripted models\n\nA plugin can declare `models` with `dal.model(id = ..., caps = ..., run = ..., uses = [...])`. A scripted model that runs where no script context exists fails with `script context is unavailable for this model`. To call a model from a tool, request `models.infer`.\n"),
    ("prompt", "# The system prompt\n\nSection order: identity, D2, tools, skills, rules, rulebook, plugins, environment, instructions, fallback, docs line.\n\nThe labeled `AGENTS.md` walk: instruction context, never a deterministic permission boundary. `SYSTEM.md` adds user text. Skill descriptions appear in the prompt or as a letter. Letters carry long stable text as images; the exact text stays readable at `letter://<id>`. The manual line lists manuals; `dal://prompt` lists the parts.\n\nFont note: this product bundles GNU Unifont 18.0.01 glyph data (`extensions/dal-ext/assets/unifont-18.0.01.hex`), used under the SIL Open Font License, Version 1.1. The full license text is in `extensions/dal-ext/assets/OFL-1.1.txt`. Upstream: https://unifoundry.com/unifont/\n"),
    ("protocol", "# The dal protocol\n\nFraming over stdio or a local socket; `initialize` negotiates capabilities. The protocol version is 1. A client that can answer approval requests lists `approval` in its `initialize` capabilities, and a client that can answer extension questions lists `question`. A session with no such client attached denies approvals and gives questions their default answer at once.\n\n| method | params | result | capability |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| initialize | protocolVersion, capabilities | ready | none |\n| protocol/schema | none | schema | none |\n| commands/list | none | commands | none |\n| session/list | limit, cursor, search | sessions | sessions |\n| session/open | ref | session | sessions |\n| session/close | sessionId | ok | sessions |\n| session/view | sessionId, limit, before | view | sessions |\n| session/subscribe | sessionId, gen, after | stream | sessions |\n| session/unsubscribe | sessionId | ok | sessions |\n| session/submit | sessionId, command | reply | sessions |\n| session/answer | sessionId, requestId, answer | ok | sessions |\n| blob/read | sessionId, blobId | bytes | blobs |\n| models/list | none | models | models |\n| docs/read | uri | text | docs |\n| host/subscribe | none | stream | host.updates |\n| host/unsubscribe | none | ok | host.updates |\n| auth/status | none | providers | auth |\n| auth/login | provider, method, apiKey | pending, then ok | auth |\n| auth/cancel | loginId | cancelled | auth |\n| auth/logout | provider | removed | auth |\n\nAn `auth/login` with `method` `browser` or `device` answers `pending` with the `loginId` and `url` (and `userCode` for `device`) as soon as the provider flow reports one, and publishes one `login_finished` host update, naming the attempt's `loginId`, when the flow ends; `method` `api_key` takes the key in `apiKey` and answers `ok` after the key is stored. `auth/cancel` takes the `loginId` of a pending login, cancels the attempt, and answers whether one was still pending. `auth/status` lists each provider as `ready`, `not_configured`, or `expired`. `auth/logout` without `provider` removes every stored credential; a `provider` that is present but not a string is refused with `-32602` and removes nothing. For `auth/login`, `auth/cancel`, `auth/logout`, `session/list`, `session/view`, `session/subscribe`, and `docs/read`, an omitted optional member selects its default while a present member of the wrong type (or non-object params) is refused with `-32602`; members that take no default stay required (`provider` and `method` for `auth/login`, `sessionId` wherever used). Invalid parameter errors format their message as `invalid params for <method>: <detail>` with code `-32602`. ACP `session/list` accepts null `cwd` and `cursor` filters. `session/submit` takes every command type the `Command` decoder knows; an unknown `type` is refused with `-32602`. `session/submit` refuses an `export` command whose path is absolute or climbs with `..`, answering `-32602` and writing no file; relative paths inside the workspace and the default target stay valid. Updates carry `seq` and `gen`; clients resync on gaps. An extension that registers a status kind reports changes as `ext_status` updates with `ext`, `state` (`busy` or `quiet`), and optional `text`; for example, `{\"type\":\"ext_status\",\"ext\":\"focus\",\"state\":\"busy\",\"text\":\"indexing\"}` and `{\"type\":\"ext_status\",\"ext\":\"focus\",\"state\":\"quiet\"}`. The host polls each kind on a short fixed interval and sends an update only when the state or text changes. A quiet extension with no text is the starting state and sends nothing until it has been busy. A fresh wire subscription has no status seed and receives changes as they occur; an embedded client reads the current busy or text-bearing statuses with `Agent::ext_status`. ACP clients receive the same change as a `_dal/notice` with `kind` `status` and the text `<ext>: <text>`, `<ext>: busy`, or `<ext>: quiet`. A2A streams carry it as the `dal.status` metadata of a status update. The router sends nothing. `dalgon --json` writes its result line only after every status kind is quiet. Requests need exactly one answer. Blobs are content-addressed. Error codes follow JSON-RPC. A server that is stopping answers each new request with `-32009` (`server_draining`), gives running requests one second to finish, and then closes the connection. `experimental/` methods may change.\n\n## Assistant entries\n\nAssistant entries carry one `model` identifier. Their optional `route` member\nidentifies the route that produced the response:\n\n| Value | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `{\"api\":\"openai_chat\"}` | An API route, with its API family. The other family values are `openai_responses`, `openai_codex`, and `anthropic`. |\n| `\"synthetic\"` | A named synthetic model. |\n| `\"harness\"` | A built-in harness mode. |\n\nThe route describes the existing `model` identifier. Its API family is independent\nof the entry's top-level `api` member, which describes the provider wire format.\nNew responses include `route`. An absent member means the route is unknown, not\nthat it is an API route. The decoder rejects `null`, unknown route kinds, and model\nidentifiers that do not fit the declared synthetic or harness kind.\n\n`protocol/schema` includes this optional, non-null member. Protocol version 1\nand the format-1 encoding of assistant entries remain unchanged. Turn-ID\nreservations use journal format 2; see [durable turn identities](sessions.md#durable-turn-identities).\n"),
    ("requirements", "# Requirements dal must meet\n\nEvery requirement in this document is a mandatory obligation that dal and dalgona must satisfy. Unmet obligations stand as requirements to implement and verify rather than claims of completed proof.\n\n## Platform and Architecture\n\ndal must be a complete and fully usable coding agent platform on its own.\ndal must maintain a minimal expandable core with no private interior.\nEvery capability beyond the core loop must register through the public extension door.\nThe primary binary must be dalgon, and dal and dl must be identical aliases.\ndalgona must extend dal through the public extension door and supply opinionated batteries.\nThe repository and all workspace packages must inherit the Sustainable Use License Version 1.0 with licensor metaphorics.\nPrebuilt binaries for Linux, macOS, and Windows on x86_64 and aarch64 must attach to release assets.\n\n## Seams and Protocols\n\nIn process, the host-agent seam must provide five operations over one snapshot: read, change stream, command path, request-response, and blob read.\nAcross processes, dal must serve a JSON-RPC protocol over standard input/output and local sockets matching the in-process seam.\ndal serve must bind only to loopback, unauthenticated by default, while public mode must require a token for remote clients arriving through a trusted HTTPS/WSS reverse proxy.\nHeadless JSON mode must emit one prompt result on success or one JSON-RPC error object on failure, without streaming handshakes.\ndal must support inline terminal, fullscreen terminal user interface, headless print, headless JSON, ACP, RPC, router, and A2A interfaces.\nThe ACP server must support protocolVersion 1 and protocolVersion 2.\nThe A2A interface must support version 1.0 over JSON-RPC and HTTP+JSON when started with the --a2a flag.\n\n## Extension and Plugin System\n\nPlugins must be evaluated from Starlark scripts at load time with no compilation step.\ndal must load user plugins exclusively from the user data directory under plugins; workspace directories must never load plugins.\nPlugins must declare required capabilities in a deny-by-default manifest, and the host must grant only user-approved capabilities.\nPlugins must be able to register tools, slash commands, skills, rules, and prompt sections through the extension door.\nFirst-party extensions must register documentation pages through the shared extension builder.\nPlugins must hook into lifecycle events with declared result types: tool calls, user input, turn boundaries, and session lifecycle.\nA plugin load failure must halt startup and report the file, line, and column.\nSlash command /reload must evaluate fresh plugins without tearing live state if evaluation fails.\nOne extension software development kit must serve dal and dalgona, so that a plugin that loads in dalgon loads in dalgona.\ndal must document a conversion workflow to port external TypeScript extensions to Starlark plugins.\n\n## Session Journal and State\n\nSessions must be stored as append-only journals synced to disk on each change, and a record must count as saved only after fsync.\nSession histories must form trees, and resuming with -r or continuing with -c must preserve tree leaves and branches.\nSlash commands /tree, /fork, and /clone must inspect and branch session histories.\nSlash command /export must write a standalone session transcript file.\nContext compaction must run automatically at 85 percent of the context window by default, running remote compaction before local summarization.\nLong or stable text may project as image letters while the exact source text must remain retrievable at letter://<id>.\n\n## Tools and Approval\n\nNormal mode must provide read, search, patch, and exec tools.\nEval-first and eval-only modes must gate tools through Starlark evaluation.\nThe eval tool must run Starlark only; an operation denied outside eval must be denied inside eval, and eval must not be treated as a sandbox.\nThe patch tool must support multiple edit styles, defaulting to anchor in dal and hashline in dalgona.\nThe patch edit style must follow the active model per request when configured in the per-model table.\nThe approval ladder must follow --approval ask|edits|all, asking before patch and exec execution by default.\nWhen no person can answer an approval prompt, dal must deny the operation and record the reason.\nRead-only tool calls must batch in parallel, while mutating patch and exec calls must run one at a time in request order.\nLong foreground commands must detach into background jobs to preserve interactive control.\nTool execution may carry scoped command prefix grants valid for the active job.\nAn optional sandbox must guard home files outside allowed paths, with no network restriction.\n\n## Code Intelligence and Syntax Operations\n\nSymbol search must extract definitions from on-disk parses using Tree-sitter across supported programming languages.\nSearch mode symbol must list matching definitions, outline single files, and reveal single matches with def tags.\nThe patch tool must support definition replacement and relative insertion by symbol name and def tag.\nHashline block edits must use the N* operator to address outermost syntactic blocks when symbol search is enabled.\ndal must not include a language server protocol client; code intelligence must consist of bounded search, syntax walks, and structural patch.\n\n## Prompt Construction and Provider Integration\n\nThe system prompt must assemble from AGENTS.md files, SYSTEM.md, active skills, rules, and plugin sections.\nTTSR stream rules must inspect model output streams, match patterns, interrupt generation, inject rules, and retry.\ndal must connect to four provider families: OpenAI Chat, OpenAI Responses, OpenAI Codex, and Anthropic.\nSession commands /model and /thinking must change the model and reasoning effort during a session.\nThinking levels must include off, minimal, low, medium, high, xhigh, and max.\nLuna Reserve must operate on the Codex provider as an opt-in model and must never be a default model.\nProvider and protocol replies must decode tolerantly to support upstream schema evolution.\n\n## Terminal User Interface\n\nText measurement must match terminal drawing cell widths and must never split multi-byte or wide characters.\nThe terminal user interface must support inline scrollback and alternate-screen fullscreen modes.\nThe terminal user interface must render native diagrams for D2, Nomnoml, DOT, and Mermaid formats.\nTerminal themes must provide sufficient contrast to satisfy accessibility contrast standards.\n\n## Self-Documentation System\n\nDocumentation must be served by one resolver over immutable pages under dal:// and dalgona:// schemes.\nDocumentation URIs must follow the grammar scheme://page with lowercase segments and no traversal syntax.\nEvery page must be valid UTF-8 Markdown with a title on line one, staying under 49152 bytes and 1500 lines.\nSlash command /docs and read tool scheme reads must render manual pages without line numbers or edit footers.\nFailed page lookups must provide nearest suggestions within an edit distance of two.\n\n## Guard and Batteries\n\nThe core must ship a lean judge component with configurable model roles, typed judgment, and auto|on|off gating.\nIn dal the judge must feed judged rules and eval batching; dalgona must enable all judge-fed capabilities by default.\ndalgona must ship batteries for web search and fetch, plan and todo, a rich questionnaire tool, an MCP client, and a review loop.\ndalgona must enable the anti-complexity and anti-slop guard by default, while dal must keep the guard disabled by default.\nThe guard must measure growth deltas, absolute metrics, metric deltas, and current bests, blocking only deterministic invalid content.\n\n## Concurrency and Resource Bounds\n\ndal must maintain structured concurrency with explicit ownership, race-free state transitions, and no leaked threads or processes.\nConcurrency invariants and lock ordering must be verifiable under deterministic permutation tests.\nConfiguration parsing must deny unknown fields and report the exact offending line in dal.toml.\nUnder high load, the system must bound memory growth, manage open file descriptors, and maintain responsive keypress echo latency.\n"),
    ("rules", "# Rules: stream, always, and rulebook\n\nThree kinds: stream rules watch output, always rules apply every turn, and rulebook files hold project rules. User rule files live under `<data-root>/rules/`; project rule files live under `<workspace>/.dal/rules/`. Enabled plugins can also supply files from their `rules/` directories. Read a rule at `rule://<name>`.\n\n## Interrupts and retries\n\nStream rules can interrupt a response and supply a reminder for the retry. `rules.max_retries` caps these interrupts per turn. At the cap, unjudged matches supply reminders without interrupting. Judged matches become report-only: they do not call the judge or add a reminder. A rule marked as report-only never interrupts.\n\nRule reminders are written to the session journal before delivery. They enter model context and its token estimate, and remain available when you resume the session. A session keeps its captured rule set; editing a rule file does not change an already-open session.\n\n## Judged rules\n\nA judged rule has a pattern and a yes-or-no judge question. Session startup checks the selected judge route and its credentials without generating a response or consuming a scripted response. Readiness does not guarantee that a later model request will succeed.\n\n`rules.judge` controls which judged rules enter the session's matching set:\n\n- `off` excludes judged rules.\n- `auto` includes them when the shared judge is ready. Otherwise, it excludes them and reports a readiness note.\n- `on` requires a ready shared judge. If none is ready, session startup fails.\n\nThe shared judge has a separate `gate` setting under `[judge]`. Setting `judge.gate = \"off\"` skips its readiness check. Setting `rules.judge = \"off\"` does not disable the shared judge, which other features can use. With either gate set to `on`, an unavailable judge prevents startup. Unexpected readiness errors are returned, not treated as missing credentials.\n\nWhen a judged match can interrupt, dal closes the provider stream before requesting the verdict:\n\n- `true` confirms a violation. dal records the rule fire and retries with its reminder.\n- `false` rejects the match. dal reports one informational pass note and retries without a rule fire or reminder.\n- A judge error or timeout reports one informational failure note and retries without a rule fire or reminder.\n\nThe rule is latched for that turn before the verdict wait, so a retry does not judge the same rule again. Cancellation stops the wait without a late rule fire or retry. The configured retry cap still applies.\n\nSee [Settings in dal.toml](config.md) for rule defaults.\n"),
    ("sandbox", "# The sandbox\n\nWith `sandbox = true` in dal.toml (or `--sandbox`), every `exec` child runs\nthrough the platform sandbox helper: Landlock on Linux through the\n`dalgon __sandbox` helper, Seatbelt on macOS, and an AppContainer on Windows.\nThe allowed roots are the workspace, the system temp directory, the platform\ncache directory, and the `sandbox_writable` entries; nothing else under the\nhome directory is writable, and there is no network restriction. The Windows\nbackend matches the read-open model as far as DACL editing safely reaches:\nthe per-run `dalgon.sandbox.<guid>` container reads and executes wherever\nsystem ACLs already permit application containers (`ALL APPLICATION\nPACKAGES`), plus the launch directory and every `PATH` directory. Each\nwritable root's ancestors get traverse access only (POSIX `+x` parity): the\ncontainer resolves through them but cannot list or read sibling files.\nA failed grant there fails the launch, since a partially installed read\npolicy is worse than none. Drive roots are never\ntouched: an inheritable ACE on a drive root would propagate to every file\non the volume. Writes stay under the granted roots, which get full\ncontrol. If the helper is missing\nor the kernel cannot enforce the sandbox, dal refuses the command with the\nsetup error instead of running it unsandboxed. It does not cover plugins\nrunning with your permissions.\n"),
    ("sdk", "# Embed dal in a Rust program\n\nUse `dal-agent` and `dal-core` through their public Rust APIs. The repository has one Cargo workspace and one `Cargo.lock`.\n\n## Run the embedding example\n\nFrom the repository root, run:\n\n```sh\ncargo run -p dalgon --example sdk\n```\n\nThe example uses a local replay fixture and needs no provider credentials. It prints `hello from scripted SDK` and shuts down the host. Read the complete implementation in [`cli/dalgon/examples/sdk/main.rs`](../cli/dalgon/examples/sdk/main.rs).\n\n## Own the session lifecycle\n\nLoad configuration with `Config::load(ConfigProduct::Dalgon, data_root, product_defaults_toml, user_toml)`. The loader takes TOML text; it does not read files or the environment. Capture those inputs at your process boundary.\n\nBuild a `Product` with its data root and extensions, and pass it with the configuration and captured `Env` to `Host::start`. Open a session with `Host::open`; use `SessionRef::Ephemeral` when the session must not be saved. `Host::close` closes one session by id, closing descendant child sessions before their parent.\n\nSubscribe before submitting a prompt. Handle a rejected submission, a closed subscription, and a resync request. Read text updates until `UpdateKind::TurnEnded`; do not wait for the subscription to close before calling `Host::shutdown`. `Host::shutdown` takes a timeout grace duration and returns a report with the closed session count. The runnable example shows each of these outcomes.\n\n## Store extension state\n\nIn saved sessions, file-backed extensions use the session sidecar service for\nsmall private values. Sidecars are unavailable in ephemeral sessions, including\nthe example above. Names must contain 1 to 64 bytes of lowercase ASCII letters,\ndigits, `.`, `_`, or `-`, and must start with a letter or digit. Sidecar files\nstay inside the owning extension's session directory, and writes are atomic.\n\n"),
    ("serve", "# dalgon serve: the model router and A2A\n\nLoopback only; `--public` stays on loopback and requires `dalgon serve token` for remote clients arriving through a trusted HTTPS/WSS reverse proxy on the same host. Routes pick model ids; continuation resumes sessions; errors name the fix. `[serve] approval` gates router actions. A2A with `--a2a` serves the agent card and methods.\n"),
    ("sessions", "# Sessions, history, and compaction\n\nSessions live under the data root. Use `-c` to continue a session or `-r` to resume one. Names label sessions. Use `/tree`, `/fork`, and `/clone` to inspect or change branches. Use `/export` to write a shareable file. Read an entry at `session://<id>/<entry>`. `--no-session` runs without saving.\n\nChild sessions also write a `dal-agent` `child_policy` record with their inherited approval mode and tool allowlist; an absent tool list means unrestricted access. Session replay requires at most one policy record; duplicate or conflicting records fail replay.\n\n\n## Durable turn identities\n\nFor saved sessions, dal writes a turn-ID reservation to the journal before it acknowledges a prompt, follow-up, or wake allocation. A record counts as saved only after it is synced to disk. The first reservation creates the journal even if the turn never starts.\n\nCancelling queued work does not release its identity. Later turns keep their allocated IDs. When a session reopens, unused reservations remain consumed. Queued text is not restored, so submit it again if you still want to run it. A reservation alone does not create a completed turn.\n\nReservation records use journal format 2. All other record kinds retain format 1, so one journal can contain both versions. Existing journals remain readable and are not rewritten. Once a format-2 reservation is written, a build that reads only format 1 cannot open that journal. Use a build that supports both formats.\n\nMulti-record batches still lack atomic framing against process crashes. An interrupted batch is not guaranteed to roll back cleanly on reopen.\n\n\n## Compaction\n\nCompaction starts by default at 85 percent of the context window, remote first. Use `/compact <focus>` to force it. Compaction retains recent entries and inserts summary text. Raw provider-native history items are dropped and are not continued into subsequent model requests.\n\n## Context measurements\n\nMeasured context use applies only to the model route that produced the latest\nassistant response on the selected branch after compaction. The route includes\nits kind and, for API routes, its API family. A matching model name alone is not\nenough.\n\nCompleted and partial responses retain this identity when the session reopens.\nIf the latest response has no route metadata, dal uses a context estimate rather\nthan an older measurement. Existing journals stay readable and are not rewritten.\nSee [assistant entries](protocol.md#assistant-entries) for the stored field.\n"),
    ("symbols", "# Symbols: search and structural editing\n\ndal and dalgona provide syntax-aware symbol search and structural block editing over supported programming languages.\nSymbol intelligence relies on Tree-sitter parsers to extract semantic definitions and syntactic blocks directly from source code.\nNo language server protocol daemon runs; symbol operations are bounded, deterministic parse passes over current file bytes.\n\n## Configuration\n\nSymbol capabilities are gated by the `search_symbols` setting in `dal.toml`.\nIn dal, `search_symbols` is `false` by default.\nIn dalgona, `search_symbols` is `true` by default.\n\n- `search: mode \"symbol\" is off. Set search_symbols = true in dal.toml.`\n- `patch: line <n>: block ops (N*) need search_symbols = true in dal.toml.`\n\n## Definition kinds\n\nSymbol extraction folds language-specific syntax nodes onto a closed set of twelve definition kinds:\n\n| Kind | Code construct | Typical scope |\n|---|---|---|\n| `function` | Free function or procedure | Module or top-level |\n| `method` | Method inside a class, impl block, trait, or receiver | Type or instance scope |\n| `struct` | Struct or union definition | Type definition |\n| `enum` | Enumeration or variant type | Type definition |\n| `trait` | Trait definition | Interface definition |\n| `impl` | Implementation block named by target type | Type implementation |\n| `module` | Module, namespace, or package | Namespace definition |\n| `macro` | Macro definition | Macro definition |\n| `const` | Constant, immutable value, or static definition | Value definition |\n| `type` | Type alias or type definition | Type definition |\n| `class` | Class definition | Object type |\n| `interface` | Interface or module type definition | Interface contract |\n\nSupported languages include Rust, TypeScript, JavaScript, Go, Python, OCaml, C, and C++.\nFiles lacking parser extractors report:\n- `search: <p> has no symbol support (<lang>).`\n- `patch: <p> has no symbol support (<lang>).`\n\n## Symbol search\n\nThe `search` tool accepts `mode: \"symbol\"` with a `pattern` string.\nThe query pipeline matches definitions from fresh parses of current file bytes.\nTrigram literals from the final segment filter candidate files, followed by fresh syntax walks.\n\n### Pattern grammar\n\n```\npattern = \"*\" / [ \"::\" ] segment *( \"::\" segment )\nsegment = name [ \"[\" ordinal \"]\" ]\nordinal = 1*digit\n```\n\n- Suffix matching: `pattern` matches any definition whose qualified name ends with the specified segments. For example, `run` matches `Engine::run` and `Worker::run`.\n- Absolute matching: A leading `::` pins the match to the complete qualified name. For example, `::Engine::run` matches only top-level `Engine::run`.\n- Ordinal disambiguation: `[k]` specifies the 1-based source-order definition among identical names in the same file. For example, `apply[2]` targets the second definition named `apply`.\n- File outline: `pattern: \"*\"` with `path` set to one file lists every definition in that file in source order.\n\nInvalid patterns return specific errors:\n- `search: invalid symbol pattern <pattern>: <reason>.`\n  Valid reason strings are `empty`, `empty segment`, `ordinal must be a number`, `ordinal must be at least 1`, or `* with more than one path`.\n- `search: pattern * needs path set to one file.`\n- `search: <p> is not a file.`\n- `search: parser failed on <p>: <msg>.`\n\n### Budgets and caps\n\n- Maximum candidate files parsed: 500 files per query.\n- Each file parse has a 250 ms wall-clock budget. A query can parse multiple files.\n- Single match reveal limit: at most 2000 lines and 50 KiB (51,200 bytes).\n- Text files up to 16 MiB are parsed; binary and UTF-16 files are skipped.\n\n### Output forms\n\nMultiple matches list matching rows followed by a guidance footer:\n```\nlib/patch.ml:40-88 function apply\nlib/patch.ml:120-130 function apply[2]\nlib/tool.ml:12-30 function Tool::apply\n[3 definitions. Search a longer name to see one whole with its tag.]\n```\n\nA single match within limits reveals the entire definition with line numbers and def tag:\n```\nlib/patch.ml:40-88 function apply tag 7C2E4B1A\n40:let apply w edits =\n...\n88:  Ok changes\n```\n\nOutline queries print definitions without the guidance footer:\n```\nlib/patch.ml:40-88 function apply\nlib/patch.ml:120-130 function apply[2]\n[2 definitions.]\n```\n\nWhen search limits are reached:\n- `[<n> definitions shown; parse budget reached.]`\n- `No definitions match <pattern> in <scope>.`\n\n## Structural block editing\n\nStructural editing enables edits anchored to definition boundaries or syntax blocks.\n\n### Patch by symbol\n\nThe `patch` tool accepts `symbol` change objects:\n- `symbol`: Definition name matching search mode symbol output (e.g., `Engine::run` or `run[2]`).\n- `tag`: Optional 8-character hexadecimal tag (`tag8(\"def:\" ++ definition_raw_bytes)`). When supplied, patch verifies that the definition has not changed since it was read.\n- `at`: Insertion position relative to definition: `\"replace\"` (default), `\"before\"`, or `\"after\"`.\n- `new`: Replacement or inserted text. Passing `new: \"\"` with `at: \"replace\"` deletes the definition.\n- `old`: When replacing text within a definition without replacing the whole block, provide `symbol`, `old`, and `new`. `old` must occur exactly once inside the definition; no `tag` is needed.\n\nSymbol patch errors:\n- `patch: changes[<i>]: tag <T> does not match <name> now (tag <U>). The file changed since you read it; read it again.`\n- `patch: changes[<i>]: definition tag needs a symbol selector.`\n- `patch: no definition <name> in <p>.`\n- `patch: <n> definitions named <name> in <p>.`\n- `patch: changes[<i>]: old is not in <name> in <p>. Copy it again from the lines below, or read <p>.`\n- `patch: <p> has no symbol support (<lang>).`\n- `patch: changes[<i>]: symbol support is not enabled for this file.`\n- `patch: parser failed on <p>: <reason>.` The reason is one of `Budget`, `TooManyParses`, `Panic`, or `Unsupported`.\n\n### Hashline N* block operator\n\nIn the `hashline` edit style, the `N*` operator references the outermost named syntactic block beginning at line `N`:\n- `PUT N*:` replaces the lines of the resolved syntactic block.\n- `CUT N*` deletes the lines of the resolved syntactic block.\n- `PUT >N*:` inserts text after the last line of the syntactic block at sibling indentation.\n\nBlock syntax errors:\n- `patch: no syntactic block begins at line <n> of <p>.`\n- `patch: line <n>: block ops (N*) need search_symbols = true in dal.toml.`\n"),
    ("terminal", "# The terminal interface and its keys\n\nInline by default; fullscreen on request. Keys:\n\n| key | action | F1 label |\n|---|---|---|\n| enter | submit | Send |\n| esc | close or interrupt | Close or interrupt |\n| up | previous history | Previous history |\n| down | next history | Next history |\n| F1 | help | Help |\n| pgup / pgdn | page through the transcript | Transcript page up / Transcript page down |\n| end | jump to the latest transcript entry | Jump to latest |\n| ctrl+t | transcript overlay (inline) | Transcript overlay |\n| ctrl+shift+f or F3 | search the transcript (fullscreen) | Search transcript |\n| shift+left | extend the fullscreen selection left | Extend selection left |\n| shift+right | extend the fullscreen selection right | Extend selection right |\n| shift+up | extend the fullscreen selection up | Extend selection up |\n| shift+down | extend the fullscreen selection down | Extend selection down |\n\nPress F1 to open keyboard help without adding it to the transcript. Use Up and\nDown to scroll one row, or PageUp and PageDown to scroll one page. Press Esc or\nCtrl+C to close help. Your draft stays unchanged, and help ignores typing and\npaste. Run `/hotkeys` instead to keep the key list in the transcript.\n\nIn fullscreen, and in the Ctrl+T overlay, the transcript viewport follows the live edge; paging up detaches it and the hint shows `following stopped · press end to jump to the latest` until End returns to the latest entry.\n\nIn fullscreen, hold Shift and use the arrow keys to select transcript text.\nPress Ctrl+C to copy the selection without interrupting a running turn.\nTyping clears the selection and returns to the composer. In inline mode, use\nyour terminal's native text selection.\n\nDiagram labels use installed fonts captured when the terminal interface starts.\nRestart after installing fonts. If a diagram cannot resolve a font, dal shows\nthe original source and a failure reason instead of an unlabeled image.\n\nIn fullscreen only, Ctrl+Shift+F (F3 on terminals without kitty keyboard) opens a filter row over the viewport top; Enter jumps to the latest match and Esc closes it.\n\nWhen the layout has room, a busy extension has a row `<extension>: <text>` (or `<extension>: busy` with no text) above the status line in the dim role and in extension-name order. When more extensions are busy than the activity rows allow, the last row reads `(<n> more extensions busy)` when multiple extensions are hidden, or `(1 more extension busy)` when one is hidden. A quiet extension has no row, and the rows follow the terminal's color and `NO_COLOR` settings. F1 opens help; steer types while dal works; follow up waits for the turn; stop ends it at once. Copy uses the terminal clipboard. The first non-empty locale selects ambiguous character width: Japanese, Chinese, and Korean select wide; other locales select narrow. Themes tune contrast; opt-in inline images show diagrams; native diagrams render as text when images are off.\n"),
    ("tools", "# Tools, modes, and approval\n\nModes: `normal`, `eval-first`, and `eval-only` list the same tools: `read`, `search`, `patch`, `exec`, and `eval`. The eval modes additionally reach plugin tools and prompt sections marked `eval_only`. Other modes hide them, and calls to hidden tools fail. Each tool takes typed parameters with limits. Edit styles: `anchor` by default; per-model table picks `replace`, `apply_patch`, `anchor`, `hashline`, `hashline-light`, or `hashline-enhanced`; tiers `simple`, `balanced`, `strict`. Search modes are `find`, `grep`, and `symbol`; no LSP. The approval ladder `--approval ask|edits|all` carries ask, edits, all; by default dal asks before every `patch` and every `exec`; when no person can answer, dal denies and says why. Approval previews show control and bidirectional characters as visible escape sequences instead of sending them to the terminal. Call-scoped argv-prefix grants allow one command prefix. Background jobs run detached; subagents are off by default in dal. Read schemes: `dal://`, plus plugin schemes.\n\n## Recover from a failed patch\n\nIf a patch fails after changing files, dal tries to restore each completed operation. If restoration fails, the error names every affected path and any retained backup. Inspect those paths and restore the affected files before retrying. Keep the backups until recovery is complete.\n\n## The eval tool\n\n`eval` runs one Starlark cell per call. Nothing but Starlark runs in it, and every effect a cell requests goes through the same approval as the tool of that name. A call you deny outside `eval` is denied inside it. `eval` is not a sandbox.\n\nThe request has one required field and two optional ones, and nothing else:\n\n```json\n{\"code\": \"tools.search(\\\"TODO\\\", path = \\\"src\\\")\", \"uses\": [\"tools.search\"], \"data\": {\"limit\": 20}}\n```\n\n- `code` is the cell.\n- `uses` narrows the operations the cell may request.\n- `data` is any JSON value. The cell reads it as `data`; it is `None` when absent.\n\nA cell inherits its operations from `eval.uses` in `dal.toml`. Leave out `uses` and the cell may request all of them. Give `uses` and the cell may request only those, and each must be in `eval.uses`; an id outside it stops the cell with `scope_exceeded`. Give `uses = []` and the cell can only compute: no tools, network, state, or child sessions. With no `eval.uses`, every cell can only compute.\n\nEach cell starts fresh. It has three names: `ctx`, `tools` (the same as `ctx.tools`), and `data`. It needs no `load` and no wrapper function, and it keeps no globals for the next cell. The last expression is the result. If the last statement is not an expression, the result is `None`. There is no `return`.\n\n```starlark\npage = tools.search(\"TODO\", path = \"src\")\n{\"count\": len(page.matches), \"truncated\": page.truncated}\n```\n\nCalls return their values directly, and a failed call stops the cell. To inspect an expected failure, pass the operation itself to `ctx.try_call`, not the result of calling it:\n\n```starlark\nr = ctx.try_call(tools.exec, \"cargo test\")\n{\"exit_ok\": r.ok}\n```\n\nDenial, cancellation, and hard limits stop the cell and cannot be caught. A patch that ran before a later failure is not undone.\n\nA pure cell reads only its data:\n\n```starlark\nsorted(data[\"names\"])\n```\n\nSend it with `\"uses\": []`. To call a plugin tool from a cell, write `tools[\"quality.todos\"](path = \"src\")`; the cell needs the tool's id and the operations it declares, as the `plugins` page explains. To run calls at the same time, open a scope with a limit from 1 to 64, then collect the results in submission order:\n\n```starlark\ns = ctx.scope(limit = 2)\nfor path in [\"src\", \"tests\"]:\n    s.tools.search(\"TODO\", path = path)\ns.all()\n```\n\nThe reply is an envelope, not just the value:\n\n| field | meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `invocation_id` | the id of this cell run |\n| `status` | `completed`, `failed`, `denied`, `cancelled`, `limit_exceeded`, or `indeterminate` |\n| `value` | the result; present only when `status` is `completed` |\n| `error` | `code`, `message`, and `stage`; present when the cell did not complete |\n| `prints` | the lines the cell printed and whether they were cut; a diagnostic channel only |\n| `cleanup` | whether dal finished cleanup, and the ids of any calls still outstanding |\n| `observer_errors` | hooks that failed while watching the cell |\n\nA `completed` status means the code finished. It does not mean the goal was met, that a test passed, or that every handled failure succeeded.\n"),
];
